WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.900
&gt;&gt; So, it's an honor to have Tom Dietterich with us today.

00:00:07.901 --> 00:00:11.760
I think I remember the first moment,

00:00:11.761 --> 00:00:15.915
I think I ran into Tom I was passing a seminar room.

00:00:15.916 --> 00:00:17.670
I looked in and then walked

00:00:17.671 --> 00:00:20.255
in at Stanford Margaret Jacks Hall,

00:00:20.256 --> 00:00:21.280
and there was some sort of a very

00:00:21.281 --> 00:00:23.810
interesting debate going on.

00:00:23.811 --> 00:00:25.240
Some machine learning topic.

00:00:25.241 --> 00:00:28.105
I think Rich's folks were there, and so,

00:00:28.106 --> 00:00:33.170
it's a just a memory that I've had over the past.

00:00:33.171 --> 00:00:34.725
I just remember that the first glimmer of

00:00:34.726 --> 00:00:36.990
the energy of Tom Dietterich.

00:00:36.991 --> 00:00:40.355
I've come to know him and love his work over the years.

00:00:40.356 --> 00:00:43.920
Tom mentioned during our fireside chat,

00:00:43.921 --> 00:00:45.510
the video we did last night together

00:00:45.511 --> 00:00:47.260
that we were talking a little

00:00:47.261 --> 00:00:50.600
bit about interests and this idea of that,

00:00:50.601 --> 00:00:51.860
I think we share

00:00:51.861 --> 00:00:54.610
one thing where we both have what we call,

00:00:54.611 --> 00:00:57.300
"Research Attention Deficit Disorder."

00:00:57.301 --> 00:01:03.590
We get very curious and excited about many topics

00:01:03.591 --> 00:01:06.650
and it's amazing to me how deep

00:01:06.651 --> 00:01:10.195
Tom goes on each of them that he attacks over the years.

00:01:10.196 --> 00:01:14.540
Tom did his undergraduate work at Oberlin,

00:01:14.541 --> 00:01:17.580
and then, did his master's at UIUC.

00:01:17.581 --> 00:01:20.265
I think you mentioned you were following at

00:01:20.266 --> 00:01:22.530
the time the line

00:01:22.531 --> 00:01:24.715
of where you can find the best computer.

00:01:24.716 --> 00:01:25.500
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:01:25.501 --> 00:01:27.520
&gt;&gt; Sticking phones into

00:01:27.521 --> 00:01:30.485
these 300 bud sockets and so on in those days.

00:01:30.486 --> 00:01:37.300
And then, did his graduate work at Stanford University,

00:01:37.301 --> 00:01:40.250
following I guess in [inaudible] after hanging out with

00:01:40.251 --> 00:01:42.360
Machalski at UIUC which

00:01:42.361 --> 00:01:45.185
got him into the machine learning world.

00:01:45.186 --> 00:01:47.920
And has worked on many

00:01:47.921 --> 00:01:51.130
different areas I think following

00:01:51.131 --> 00:01:53.200
all the recent trends and leading some of them.

00:01:53.201 --> 00:01:55.570
Remember, you and I were at the first UAI conference

00:01:55.571 --> 00:01:57.060
in 1985 together.

00:01:57.061 --> 00:02:00.090
And I remember you wearing a pair of

00:02:00.091 --> 00:02:02.170
shorts and asking hard questions

00:02:02.171 --> 00:02:03.910
in front of the room back in those days.

00:02:03.911 --> 00:02:06.100
I cannot forget anything Tom

00:02:06.101 --> 00:02:08.760
about these interesting interactions

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:10.760
we've had over the years.

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:14.800
Going to the probabilistic graphical model phase,

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:16.935
it's always been touching on applications.

00:02:16.936 --> 00:02:22.890
Has been quite a lot of work during the Caelo project on

00:02:22.891 --> 00:02:24.640
desktop events and understand

00:02:24.641 --> 00:02:26.630
trying to infer the intentions

00:02:26.631 --> 00:02:31.355
of goals of users from desktop streams of evidence.

00:02:31.356 --> 00:02:34.990
More recently has been written like

00:02:34.991 --> 00:02:38.740
several of us here at the challenge of AI

00:02:38.741 --> 00:02:40.680
in the open world issues about

00:02:40.681 --> 00:02:46.460
robustness and understandability of these algorithms.

00:02:46.461 --> 00:02:49.490
He was a leader in this whole area

00:02:49.491 --> 00:02:51.800
that's now called Computational Sustainability,

00:02:51.801 --> 00:02:54.950
working with Carla Gomes at Cornell at defining this area

00:02:54.951 --> 00:02:59.240
and somehow convincing the National Science Foundation

00:02:59.241 --> 00:03:01.580
to fund it deeply.

00:03:01.581 --> 00:03:05.160
Looking at applications of optimization and

00:03:05.161 --> 00:03:10.290
other areas of Machine Intelligence to hard challenges

00:03:10.291 --> 00:03:14.620
with environment and speciation

00:03:14.621 --> 00:03:19.900
and resource management of planetary resources,

00:03:19.901 --> 00:03:24.590
sort of resonating with our AI effort now at Microsoft.

00:03:25.630 --> 00:03:28.450
Today, he'll be talking about a piece of

00:03:28.451 --> 00:03:30.420
this interesting area on robustness,

00:03:30.421 --> 00:03:32.670
I think it's an important aspect anomaly

00:03:32.671 --> 00:03:36.490
detection algorithms explanations and application.

00:03:36.491 --> 00:03:38.300
So, Tom thanks very much for being with us.

00:03:38.301 --> 00:03:41.650
&gt;&gt; Thank you. Is this working yet?

00:03:41.651 --> 00:03:42.740
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:03:42.741 --> 00:03:46.815
&gt;&gt; Can you hear me?

00:03:46.816 --> 00:03:47.535
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:03:47.536 --> 00:03:50.030
&gt;&gt; Okay. So of course,

00:03:50.031 --> 00:03:52.330
everything that I'm talking

00:03:52.331 --> 00:03:54.935
about today was done by someone else.

00:03:54.936 --> 00:03:58.085
So, this is a collaboration with

00:03:58.086 --> 00:04:01.330
two of my fellow faculty in Computer Science,

00:04:01.331 --> 00:04:03.555
Alan Fern and Weng-Keen Wong.

00:04:03.556 --> 00:04:06.080
Weng-Keen is currently rotating at NSF

00:04:06.081 --> 00:04:09.650
as a program manager but he'll be back in the Fall.

00:04:09.651 --> 00:04:11.920
And then two faculty from statistics,

00:04:11.921 --> 00:04:15.780
Sharmodeep Battacharyya and Debashis Mondal.

00:04:15.781 --> 00:04:17.970
And at least one of them did their thesis at

00:04:17.971 --> 00:04:21.100
UW but I don't remember which one.

00:04:21.101 --> 00:04:23.100
And then, several graduate students and

00:04:23.101 --> 00:04:25.230
as their contributions come up,

00:04:25.231 --> 00:04:27.460
I'll be pointing those out in the talk.

00:04:27.461 --> 00:04:30.850
So, I have a sort of long agenda here,

00:04:30.851 --> 00:04:32.050
this is more than I would

00:04:32.051 --> 00:04:33.600
usually try to cover in one talk,

00:04:33.601 --> 00:04:36.765
but some of them are fairly straightforward.

00:04:36.766 --> 00:04:38.480
So, what I want to do is start by

00:04:38.481 --> 00:04:40.470
defining this question of Anomaly Detection,

00:04:40.471 --> 00:04:42.055
what do we mean by that because

00:04:42.056 --> 00:04:44.720
that's not a terribly well-defined term.

00:04:44.721 --> 00:04:46.790
And then, I'll report

00:04:46.791 --> 00:04:50.490
briefly review big bench marking effort we did.

00:04:50.491 --> 00:04:52.650
Mostly, in the knowledge discovery

00:04:52.651 --> 00:04:53.870
and database community,

00:04:53.871 --> 00:04:56.260
there have been many algorithms

00:04:56.261 --> 00:04:59.090
published in the anomaly detection area.

00:04:59.091 --> 00:05:01.310
I think largely because they have

00:05:01.311 --> 00:05:04.810
many business problems in fraud detection and management,

00:05:04.811 --> 00:05:08.020
and this is a big tool there.

00:05:08.021 --> 00:05:14.130
But a lot of those are just publishing an algorithm,

00:05:14.131 --> 00:05:16.750
a mechanism without any kind of a theoretical foundation.

00:05:16.751 --> 00:05:18.910
And so, we wanted to understand them, and then,

00:05:18.911 --> 00:05:21.885
we've done a bit of work on

00:05:21.886 --> 00:05:24.700
probably approximately correct type of

00:05:24.701 --> 00:05:26.935
machine learning theory for

00:05:26.936 --> 00:05:29.780
what we call rare pattern anomaly detection.

00:05:29.781 --> 00:05:36.140
Then, I'll talk about scenarios like fraud detection,

00:05:36.141 --> 00:05:40.155
where you have a ranked list

00:05:40.156 --> 00:05:45.055
of anomalous transactions or users or whatever and now,

00:05:45.056 --> 00:05:46.890
you have an analyst in the loop.

00:05:46.891 --> 00:05:49.320
And so, you want to show the analysts say,

00:05:49.321 --> 00:05:51.855
your number one candidate and

00:05:51.856 --> 00:05:55.015
explain to them why you think it's anomalous.

00:05:55.016 --> 00:05:56.575
Obviously, it's not sufficient to dump

00:05:56.576 --> 00:05:58.745
180 element vector on them and say,

00:05:58.746 --> 00:06:01.880
we think this is an anonymous user, but we want.

00:06:01.881 --> 00:06:02.960
So, I'll talk about

00:06:02.961 --> 00:06:06.195
some very simple feature-based explanations.

00:06:06.196 --> 00:06:09.630
And then, if we've got the analyst in the loop,

00:06:09.631 --> 00:06:11.940
we can also get feedback from them and use that to

00:06:11.941 --> 00:06:14.430
re-rank our candidates as we're presenting them.

00:06:14.431 --> 00:06:15.980
And that turns out at least in

00:06:15.981 --> 00:06:18.545
our benchmarking to be really powerful.

00:06:18.546 --> 00:06:21.150
And then, I'll wrap up with two applications.

00:06:21.151 --> 00:06:23.110
One which is a big sort

00:06:23.111 --> 00:06:26.424
of sustainability application involving,

00:06:26.425 --> 00:06:30.390
deploying 20,000 sensors weather stations in

00:06:30.391 --> 00:06:32.990
sub-Saharan Africa and then trying to automatically

00:06:32.991 --> 00:06:36.360
detect when stations break and sensors break and so on.

00:06:36.361 --> 00:06:39.885
And the other is, sort of open world AI problem,

00:06:39.886 --> 00:06:41.395
which is the problem of

00:06:41.396 --> 00:06:43.755
open category classification

00:06:43.756 --> 00:06:46.860
in say multi-class classification.

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:49.995
So, defining anomalies.

00:06:49.996 --> 00:06:51.430
So, we're going to assume that

00:06:51.431 --> 00:06:53.840
our data are just feature vectors in

00:06:53.841 --> 00:06:57.040
dimensional real space and that we have

00:06:57.041 --> 00:06:58.650
a data set that is a mixture of

00:06:58.651 --> 00:07:00.380
what we'll call the nominal points,

00:07:00.381 --> 00:07:02.105
so that we don't call them the normal

00:07:02.106 --> 00:07:06.564
points and the others will call the anomalies.

00:07:06.565 --> 00:07:10.070
Our assumption is that the anomalies are generated by

00:07:10.071 --> 00:07:13.875
different generative process than the nominal points.

00:07:13.876 --> 00:07:17.230
But typically our data will be unlabeled,

00:07:17.231 --> 00:07:18.860
we'll talk about that I guess on

00:07:18.861 --> 00:07:22.140
the next slide which is there are really three setting.

00:07:22.141 --> 00:07:24.180
So, in some cases people have tried

00:07:24.181 --> 00:07:26.320
to formulate this as a Supervised Learning problem,

00:07:26.321 --> 00:07:28.730
so we get some labeled data,

00:07:28.731 --> 00:07:30.890
but in most of

00:07:30.891 --> 00:07:33.270
the sort of anomaly detection applications,

00:07:33.271 --> 00:07:35.745
the anomalies are quite rare.

00:07:35.746 --> 00:07:38.390
Fortunately, 50 percent of

00:07:38.391 --> 00:07:40.995
our users are not trying to defraud us,

00:07:40.996 --> 00:07:44.110
although, maybe it seems like that at times.

00:07:44.111 --> 00:07:46.590
And so, here we have severe class imbalance if we

00:07:46.591 --> 00:07:49.690
approach things from that perspective.

00:07:49.691 --> 00:07:51.450
Another possibility is that we are

00:07:51.451 --> 00:07:52.960
training on clean data that

00:07:52.961 --> 00:07:56.140
has no anomalies in it, but then,

00:07:56.141 --> 00:07:57.850
maybe at test time we are going to

00:07:57.851 --> 00:07:59.700
get contaminated data and we need to

00:07:59.701 --> 00:08:01.940
identify those contaminants and that's what

00:08:01.941 --> 00:08:04.475
happens in the open category case.

00:08:04.476 --> 00:08:06.160
And then, another situation is

00:08:06.161 --> 00:08:08.850
where our training data is contaminated with

00:08:08.851 --> 00:08:10.840
some kinds of anomalies and we

00:08:10.841 --> 00:08:13.965
want to figure out which points those might be.

00:08:13.966 --> 00:08:16.100
So, we'll be looking at those.

00:08:16.101 --> 00:08:19.840
So, I think a very important consideration

00:08:19.841 --> 00:08:23.430
here is this assumption I call the WDAD assumption.

00:08:23.431 --> 00:08:25.470
The assumption that they anomalies are drawn

00:08:25.471 --> 00:08:27.995
from a well-defined probability distribution.

00:08:27.996 --> 00:08:29.630
So, if just to backup,

00:08:29.631 --> 00:08:30.810
if you're formulating things in

00:08:30.811 --> 00:08:32.850
the supervised learning way you are

00:08:32.851 --> 00:08:34.890
essentially assuming my anomalies

00:08:34.891 --> 00:08:36.850
are coming from some well-defined distribution,

00:08:36.851 --> 00:08:39.220
right, and I can learn about that.

00:08:39.221 --> 00:08:41.400
But if we're in an adversarial setting

00:08:41.401 --> 00:08:44.070
like in the fraud or cyber security kinds of

00:08:44.071 --> 00:08:47.890
settings then that really

00:08:47.891 --> 00:08:49.695
is not a valid assumption to make, right?

00:08:49.696 --> 00:08:52.090
In fact, our adversaries are constantly trying to defeat

00:08:52.091 --> 00:08:55.295
whatever distributional assumptions we've made.

00:08:55.296 --> 00:08:58.515
Another case, in the weather network case

00:08:58.516 --> 00:09:02.175
it seems like it's adversarial in the sense that,

00:09:02.176 --> 00:09:05.200
it's sort of the number of ways something can be

00:09:05.201 --> 00:09:08.405
anomalous is sort of an unbounded set and

00:09:08.406 --> 00:09:10.270
I guess technically speaking it's still

00:09:10.271 --> 00:09:12.565
a well defined probability distribution but you don't

00:09:12.566 --> 00:09:15.320
have nearly enough data to have sampled the whole thing.

00:09:15.321 --> 00:09:18.110
So, you really can't model it.

00:09:18.111 --> 00:09:19.990
And another case that's come up,

00:09:19.991 --> 00:09:22.950
I've collaborated a little bit with Kiri Wagstaff,

00:09:22.951 --> 00:09:25.040
the Jet Propulsion Laboratory.

00:09:25.041 --> 00:09:28.910
And their user is

00:09:28.911 --> 00:09:31.510
usually a scientist and they're looking at data

00:09:31.511 --> 00:09:33.900
coming from some sensor like she's worked on

00:09:33.901 --> 00:09:35.490
some spectrometers that are

00:09:35.491 --> 00:09:38.060
on the Mars science observatory.

00:09:38.061 --> 00:09:41.430
And what the expert

00:09:41.431 --> 00:09:44.370
wants is to find the interesting data points.

00:09:44.371 --> 00:09:46.370
And now, the first few interesting data points are

00:09:46.371 --> 00:09:48.750
actually ways that the instrument fails,

00:09:48.751 --> 00:09:50.295
and they need to understand those.

00:09:50.296 --> 00:09:53.025
But then, they start being interested in

00:09:53.026 --> 00:09:55.080
unusual spectra that might

00:09:55.081 --> 00:09:56.905
signal some interesting compounds.

00:09:56.906 --> 00:09:58.670
But then, once they've seen those, then they say,

00:09:58.671 --> 00:10:01.075
well don't show me any more of those I want to see,

00:10:01.076 --> 00:10:03.020
show me real anomalies and so,

00:10:03.021 --> 00:10:05.510
it's a time changing notion.

00:10:05.511 --> 00:10:11.765
So I think, we need to be sensitive to

00:10:11.766 --> 00:10:14.842
when it's appropriate to make this assumption.

00:10:14.843 --> 00:10:17.460
So there are

00:10:17.461 --> 00:10:21.060
two main strategies for Unsupervised Anomaly Detection.

00:10:21.061 --> 00:10:24.575
It depends a lot on this parameter that we call Alpha,

00:10:24.576 --> 00:10:27.415
which is the fraction of the training points or

00:10:27.416 --> 00:10:30.840
test points that will be these anomalies.

00:10:30.841 --> 00:10:32.650
If Alpha is reasonably large,

00:10:32.651 --> 00:10:35.695
then we can think about fitting something like

00:10:35.696 --> 00:10:37.635
a two component mixture model and actually trying

00:10:37.636 --> 00:10:39.815
to model that anomaly distribution,

00:10:39.816 --> 00:10:41.120
if we're willing to make

00:10:41.121 --> 00:10:44.700
this well-defined distribution assumption.

00:10:44.701 --> 00:10:48.995
There are various conditions for that to work.

00:10:48.996 --> 00:10:52.735
Alternatively, if the proportion of anomalies is very

00:10:52.736 --> 00:10:57.405
small as it typically is in these fraud cases,

00:10:57.406 --> 00:10:59.575
then the main strategy is

00:10:59.576 --> 00:11:01.860
to treat it as a problem of outlier detection.

00:11:01.861 --> 00:11:03.675
So we're going to try to

00:11:03.676 --> 00:11:07.755
model the distribution of the nominal points,

00:11:07.756 --> 00:11:10.600
the ordinary users, and

00:11:10.601 --> 00:11:13.760
then treat the outliers as our candidate anomalies.

00:11:13.761 --> 00:11:15.625
The advantage there is that we don't have to make

00:11:15.626 --> 00:11:18.545
any assumption about the distribution.

00:11:18.546 --> 00:11:23.530
But of course, if the anomalies are not outliers,

00:11:23.531 --> 00:11:27.475
then we will miss some of them.

00:11:27.476 --> 00:11:28.805
Also, if the nominal distribution

00:11:28.806 --> 00:11:30.555
itself has very heavy tails so that it

00:11:30.556 --> 00:11:31.635
just naturally has a lot of

00:11:31.636 --> 00:11:34.795
outliers, this won't work either.

00:11:35.455 --> 00:11:40.600
So as I mentioned in the KDD literature,

00:11:40.601 --> 00:11:42.075
there must be

00:11:42.076 --> 00:11:44.765
20 or 30 different algorithms that have been published.

00:11:44.766 --> 00:11:48.760
There's a huge number of papers there.

00:11:48.761 --> 00:11:51.740
A problem with many of these is that,

00:11:51.741 --> 00:11:53.815
unlike in a lot of other parts of

00:11:53.816 --> 00:11:54.955
machine learning and Computer

00:11:54.956 --> 00:11:56.775
Vision and natural language processing,

00:11:56.776 --> 00:11:59.275
there's not a large collection of

00:11:59.276 --> 00:12:01.635
standard benchmark datasets that

00:12:01.636 --> 00:12:04.360
you could do a thorough evaluation on these algorithms.

00:12:04.361 --> 00:12:07.775
Often, there's some sort

00:12:07.776 --> 00:12:08.885
of a corporate application with

00:12:08.886 --> 00:12:11.565
a proprietary dataset that they can't share.

00:12:11.566 --> 00:12:15.060
Then the most popular benchmark dataset is

00:12:15.061 --> 00:12:17.935
this computer intrusion dataset that

00:12:17.936 --> 00:12:19.535
was collected as part of

00:12:19.536 --> 00:12:22.380
a DARPA project back in the late 90s,

00:12:22.381 --> 00:12:24.530
and it turns out to be extremely easy.

00:12:24.531 --> 00:12:29.085
So, we strongly believe in benchmarking

00:12:29.086 --> 00:12:30.125
and the need to have a

00:12:30.126 --> 00:12:31.855
large and constantly growing collection

00:12:31.856 --> 00:12:33.510
of anomaly benchmarks.

00:12:33.511 --> 00:12:36.235
So, my student Andrew Emmott

00:12:36.236 --> 00:12:39.485
conducted a big benchmarking study.

00:12:39.486 --> 00:12:42.915
Our methodology was to repurpose existing

00:12:42.916 --> 00:12:44.295
supervised learning benchmarks

00:12:44.296 --> 00:12:47.260
either classification or regression problems.

00:12:47.261 --> 00:12:51.430
So we chose 19 datasets from the UC Irvine repository,

00:12:51.431 --> 00:12:52.735
which were all the datasets that

00:12:52.736 --> 00:12:55.385
satisfied a set of criteria we had.

00:12:55.386 --> 00:12:57.135
We wanted real-valued features.

00:12:57.136 --> 00:13:00.195
We wanted enough data. We wanted enough dimension.

00:13:00.196 --> 00:13:02.625
Then we would choose one or more of the classes

00:13:02.626 --> 00:13:04.585
to be these anomaly classes,

00:13:04.586 --> 00:13:05.855
the source of our anomalies,

00:13:05.856 --> 00:13:08.035
and the rest to be the nominals.

00:13:08.036 --> 00:13:12.255
This ensures that our anomaly points are being created by

00:13:12.256 --> 00:13:14.235
some process that's different from

00:13:14.236 --> 00:13:17.475
the data that's creating the nominals.

00:13:17.476 --> 00:13:20.895
Then we systematically varied things like Alpha,

00:13:20.896 --> 00:13:23.564
the relative frequency of the anomalies,

00:13:23.565 --> 00:13:25.265
the difficulty of the points,

00:13:25.266 --> 00:13:27.465
like how deeply embedded are they inside

00:13:27.466 --> 00:13:28.865
our nominal distribution versus

00:13:28.866 --> 00:13:31.150
how much are they outliers.

00:13:31.151 --> 00:13:36.400
We added redundant or irrelevant features

00:13:36.401 --> 00:13:39.380
to see how much that could defeat the algorithms.

00:13:39.381 --> 00:13:44.100
We also looked at how clustered the anomaly points are.

00:13:44.101 --> 00:13:46.895
Because to a large extent,

00:13:46.896 --> 00:13:48.915
these anomaly detection algorithms are all

00:13:48.916 --> 00:13:50.375
something that some form

00:13:50.376 --> 00:13:52.140
of approximate density estimation.

00:13:52.141 --> 00:13:53.915
You're essentially trying to model

00:13:53.916 --> 00:13:56.720
the probability density of the nominal points,

00:13:56.721 --> 00:13:58.895
and then score points according to how

00:13:58.896 --> 00:14:01.865
the very low density points are your anomalies.

00:14:01.866 --> 00:14:03.625
Now, if the anomalies are all tightly clustered,

00:14:03.626 --> 00:14:05.500
then they form a little peak of density,

00:14:05.501 --> 00:14:07.045
they don't look like they're low-density

00:14:07.046 --> 00:14:08.735
anymore, and so you'll miss them.

00:14:08.736 --> 00:14:11.585
So, we were interested in that.

00:14:11.586 --> 00:14:13.365
We created 20 replicates for

00:14:13.366 --> 00:14:16.485
each configuration of these manipulated values,

00:14:16.486 --> 00:14:18.375
and that gave us about 25,000

00:14:18.376 --> 00:14:20.815
different benchmark datasets.

00:14:20.816 --> 00:14:26.480
So then, we compared a set of eight different algorithms.

00:14:26.481 --> 00:14:27.785
I won't go through all of them.

00:14:27.786 --> 00:14:29.415
But basically, there are some

00:14:29.416 --> 00:14:31.515
that are doing density estimation,

00:14:31.516 --> 00:14:34.345
some that we call the quantile-based methods,

00:14:34.346 --> 00:14:36.225
like the one-class Support Vector Machine

00:14:36.226 --> 00:14:38.230
is probably the best known of those.

00:14:38.231 --> 00:14:40.305
It tries to find a boundary

00:14:40.306 --> 00:14:42.525
around your data that contains say,

00:14:42.526 --> 00:14:44.065
one minus Delta of

00:14:44.066 --> 00:14:47.330
your data for some quantile you choose Delta.

00:14:47.331 --> 00:14:50.405
There are nearest neighbor-type distance methods,

00:14:50.406 --> 00:14:52.265
and then there are these projection methods.

00:14:52.266 --> 00:14:53.445
I want to talk about these

00:14:53.446 --> 00:14:55.005
because these two algorithms turn

00:14:55.006 --> 00:14:58.435
out to be very attractive for many reasons.

00:14:58.436 --> 00:15:00.395
So, the first algorithm and

00:15:00.396 --> 00:15:01.835
the one that actually performed the best on

00:15:01.836 --> 00:15:03.525
our benchmarking exercise is

00:15:03.526 --> 00:15:05.510
something called the Isolation Forest.

00:15:05.511 --> 00:15:09.185
Those of you who know me,

00:15:09.186 --> 00:15:11.090
know I love decision trees and

00:15:11.091 --> 00:15:13.475
anything random forests boosting all these things.

00:15:13.476 --> 00:15:16.555
So, I had a natural affinity for this algorithm.

00:15:16.556 --> 00:15:19.425
Although, when I first saw this paper came out,

00:15:19.426 --> 00:15:21.220
I said, "This is such a weird idea."

00:15:21.221 --> 00:15:23.115
But the basic idea is we're going to construct

00:15:23.116 --> 00:15:27.190
a fully completely random binary tree.

00:15:27.191 --> 00:15:30.380
Again, recall that our data are not labeled,

00:15:30.381 --> 00:15:33.860
so we just choose one of the attributes at random.

00:15:33.861 --> 00:15:36.685
Then, we choose a splitting threshold

00:15:36.686 --> 00:15:38.415
uniformly at random out of

00:15:38.416 --> 00:15:40.595
the observed range of the data.

00:15:40.596 --> 00:15:44.170
Then we do that, split the data recursively.

00:15:44.171 --> 00:15:48.250
We split this in some sense overfitting radically,

00:15:48.251 --> 00:15:52.535
until every data point is in its own leaf in the tree.

00:15:52.536 --> 00:15:54.070
In the paper, actually,

00:15:54.071 --> 00:15:57.140
they have ways of avoiding having to do that.

00:15:57.141 --> 00:15:59.915
You can stop the splitting at some point.

00:15:59.916 --> 00:16:02.835
The idea, and we'll build an ensemble of these,

00:16:02.836 --> 00:16:04.815
and these statistics were interested and is actually

00:16:04.816 --> 00:16:08.255
the depth of a point in the tree,

00:16:08.256 --> 00:16:10.635
the depth at which a point becomes completely isolated.

00:16:10.636 --> 00:16:13.835
Like let's say that my one data point x_i,

00:16:13.836 --> 00:16:16.380
is the only data point in this leaf node here,

00:16:16.381 --> 00:16:17.915
then we would say x_i,

00:16:17.916 --> 00:16:20.195
is isolated at depth three.

00:16:20.196 --> 00:16:22.985
The intuition here is in some sense,

00:16:22.986 --> 00:16:24.375
we're just throwing like

00:16:24.376 --> 00:16:28.745
a dartboard random separators at the data.

00:16:28.746 --> 00:16:31.525
How many random separations do we have to

00:16:31.526 --> 00:16:32.925
do before a data point is

00:16:32.926 --> 00:16:34.745
separated from all the rest of the data?

00:16:34.746 --> 00:16:36.215
The intuition is, well,

00:16:36.216 --> 00:16:37.760
if you're an outlier,

00:16:37.761 --> 00:16:40.115
it's going to take only a few random

00:16:40.116 --> 00:16:42.105
shots and we'll get you isolated.

00:16:42.106 --> 00:16:43.675
Whereas if you're deeply embedded

00:16:43.676 --> 00:16:45.415
in a tight cluster, I may have to split,

00:16:45.416 --> 00:16:49.005
split, split, split forever to get down to isolating you.

00:16:49.006 --> 00:16:51.375
So, the anomaly score that we get, well,

00:16:51.376 --> 00:16:54.360
so d-bar is going to be the average isolation depth

00:16:54.361 --> 00:16:58.710
over the 100 trees say some ensemble.

00:16:58.711 --> 00:17:03.665
Then, one nice thing in this paper is that

00:17:03.666 --> 00:17:06.545
they calculate the expected isolation depth

00:17:06.546 --> 00:17:09.035
from a little bit of theory.

00:17:09.036 --> 00:17:10.845
So, then they can normalize this so

00:17:10.846 --> 00:17:12.650
that it lies in a zero-one scale,

00:17:12.651 --> 00:17:16.585
where anomalies are given a score as close to one,

00:17:16.586 --> 00:17:18.905
and the nominal points typically have scores

00:17:18.906 --> 00:17:22.265
less than 0.5 or around 0.5.

00:17:23.405 --> 00:17:26.445
The other algorithm I want to talk about. Yes.

00:17:26.446 --> 00:17:31.935
&gt;&gt; Does that favor a particular basis of the features?

00:17:31.936 --> 00:17:35.305
If you have a linear transform of the features,

00:17:35.306 --> 00:17:37.325
you get a different basis.

00:17:37.326 --> 00:17:39.355
This is invariant.

00:17:39.356 --> 00:17:43.290
For example, if you have

00:17:43.291 --> 00:17:48.595
the point is not obviously outlier in one basis.

00:17:48.596 --> 00:17:49.830
But if you transform it,

00:17:49.831 --> 00:17:51.715
for example, even like the sphere.

00:17:51.716 --> 00:17:53.795
Then like it's fuzzy around that.

00:17:53.796 --> 00:17:55.530
I don't know if you can tell just

00:17:55.531 --> 00:17:58.480
by looking at this via one basis?

00:17:58.481 --> 00:18:00.935
&gt;&gt; It's true. So, of course,

00:18:00.936 --> 00:18:03.145
we've also looked at randomly rotating

00:18:03.146 --> 00:18:05.815
the data before we top it up.

00:18:05.816 --> 00:18:07.905
It helps a little bit, but

00:18:07.906 --> 00:18:10.445
the randomness increases the variance

00:18:10.446 --> 00:18:11.535
of the isolation depth,

00:18:11.536 --> 00:18:13.270
so then we have to make the ensemble bigger.

00:18:13.271 --> 00:18:17.145
So, it hasn't been enough of a game to be

00:18:17.146 --> 00:18:18.670
worthwhile to us because

00:18:18.671 --> 00:18:21.025
the memory starts to be an issue here.

00:18:21.026 --> 00:18:23.050
&gt;&gt; Why this business problem

00:18:23.051 --> 00:18:24.870
arise frequently enough in nature,

00:18:24.871 --> 00:18:25.995
or like in practice

00:18:25.996 --> 00:18:27.830
that you should actually care about this?

00:18:27.831 --> 00:18:30.505
&gt;&gt; Well, so in nature, I don't know.

00:18:30.506 --> 00:18:32.585
In our benchmark datasets and

00:18:32.586 --> 00:18:34.895
in our fraud detection applications,

00:18:34.896 --> 00:18:37.255
the features are generally meaningful.

00:18:37.256 --> 00:18:40.175
We're not doing this on images or things like this,

00:18:40.176 --> 00:18:44.740
where pixel 25 has no particular meaning at all.

00:18:44.741 --> 00:18:46.695
So, yeah.

00:18:46.696 --> 00:18:48.395
So I certainly wouldn't use this out of

00:18:48.396 --> 00:18:50.345
the box on images, that would be crazy.

00:18:50.346 --> 00:18:52.320
Maybe I would use it in the penultimate layer

00:18:52.321 --> 00:18:53.765
of CNN or something.

00:18:53.766 --> 00:18:56.365
I'm not even convinced that would be

00:18:56.366 --> 00:19:00.595
a good idea, but yeah.

00:19:00.596 --> 00:19:03.080
&gt;&gt; Maybe something like

00:19:03.081 --> 00:19:05.650
relationship between variables, like say,

00:19:05.651 --> 00:19:08.155
x squared plus y plus z squared,

00:19:08.156 --> 00:19:11.355
is like the meaningful variable like the outlier.

00:19:11.356 --> 00:19:15.680
&gt;&gt; But usually, just as decision trees,

00:19:15.681 --> 00:19:18.755
basically are looking at higher-order interactions.

00:19:18.756 --> 00:19:20.545
As you go deeper, you're looking,

00:19:20.546 --> 00:19:23.695
these things pretty much follow the data distribution.

00:19:23.696 --> 00:19:26.215
We only put splits where we have data,

00:19:26.216 --> 00:19:28.965
so they do a pretty good job.

00:19:28.966 --> 00:19:33.620
There was an interesting paper by Nina Mishra I think,

00:19:33.621 --> 00:19:36.760
and colleagues at ICML a couple of years ago,

00:19:36.761 --> 00:19:38.735
where they analyzed a slight variant of

00:19:38.736 --> 00:19:43.105
this algorithm and showed that

00:19:43.106 --> 00:19:46.085
the isolation depth is related to

00:19:46.086 --> 00:19:47.685
an LP norm and

00:19:47.686 --> 00:19:50.175
the distance between the outlier and the data.

00:19:50.176 --> 00:19:52.395
So in this case, basically an L1 norm.

00:19:52.396 --> 00:19:53.665
You can sort of imagine how this would

00:19:53.666 --> 00:19:56.135
be sort of like an L1 distance.

00:19:57.985 --> 00:20:04.249
If you look at these different algorithms,

00:20:04.250 --> 00:20:05.845
I think a lot of them are essentially

00:20:05.846 --> 00:20:07.125
doing something that's very similar.

00:20:07.126 --> 00:20:08.285
I mean, the nearest neighbor algorithms

00:20:08.286 --> 00:20:09.810
are calculating some distance,

00:20:09.811 --> 00:20:11.475
and the Isolation Forest is

00:20:11.476 --> 00:20:13.780
definitely calculating something like a distance also.

00:20:13.781 --> 00:20:17.125
Clearly, these things are using kernel distance,

00:20:17.126 --> 00:20:19.155
and these things are using something like

00:20:19.156 --> 00:20:21.945
a Gaussian kernel in the original space.

00:20:21.946 --> 00:20:25.345
So yeah, of course,

00:20:25.346 --> 00:20:26.845
you could apply PCA to try

00:20:26.846 --> 00:20:32.230
to rectify the data in some sense.

00:20:32.231 --> 00:20:35.020
The other method that I want to mention,

00:20:35.021 --> 00:20:39.110
or I should say, are there any other questions? Yes.

00:20:39.111 --> 00:20:43.305
&gt;&gt; Do any your benchmark datasets include

00:20:43.306 --> 00:20:47.055
behavioral data or sequential data that was generated by

00:20:47.056 --> 00:20:48.655
capturing traces of someone's behavior

00:20:48.656 --> 00:20:50.325
like networking intruder's behavior?

00:20:50.326 --> 00:20:52.270
&gt;&gt; No.

00:20:52.271 --> 00:20:55.245
So when we apply this in this DARPA,

00:20:55.246 --> 00:20:58.185
we were in this DARPA program called Atoms.

00:20:58.555 --> 00:21:02.580
There, we were looking at

00:21:02.581 --> 00:21:06.155
an organization that had 5,000 employees.

00:21:06.156 --> 00:21:09.045
We had actual desktop spyware installed,

00:21:09.046 --> 00:21:10.515
monitoring software on

00:21:10.516 --> 00:21:12.145
their desktops that they were working

00:21:12.146 --> 00:21:13.655
for some organization in

00:21:13.656 --> 00:21:16.955
the military industrial complex, so I'm allowed to say.

00:21:18.495 --> 00:21:22.645
So, we could go down and really look at

00:21:22.646 --> 00:21:24.185
every email messages they sent

00:21:24.186 --> 00:21:26.640
and even the content and all that stuff.

00:21:26.641 --> 00:21:31.245
But we summarized that at a daily time step,

00:21:31.246 --> 00:21:32.775
and turned it into a vector

00:21:32.776 --> 00:21:35.045
of how many emails did they sent,

00:21:35.046 --> 00:21:36.295
how many websites did they visit,

00:21:36.296 --> 00:21:38.945
how many this and that, and the other thing.

00:21:38.946 --> 00:21:42.585
Then we also did some normalization to try to deal with

00:21:42.586 --> 00:21:45.375
both asking how unusual

00:21:45.376 --> 00:21:47.895
was this day compared to the typical day of this user,

00:21:47.896 --> 00:21:50.385
and how unusual is this user on

00:21:50.386 --> 00:21:51.405
this day compared to everybody

00:21:51.406 --> 00:21:53.045
else in the company on that day.

00:21:53.046 --> 00:21:55.970
Because otherwise, we had these things where

00:21:55.971 --> 00:21:58.855
some new web link would be sent

00:21:58.856 --> 00:22:00.995
out through the whole company and everybody go visit it,

00:22:00.996 --> 00:22:03.125
and nobody had ever visited that link before,

00:22:03.126 --> 00:22:04.365
and we will get all excited

00:22:04.366 --> 00:22:06.250
if we're only looking at one you user.

00:22:06.251 --> 00:22:07.865
So we had to do

00:22:07.866 --> 00:22:09.235
some pretty serious feature engineering

00:22:09.236 --> 00:22:10.255
to turn this into something that

00:22:10.256 --> 00:22:15.125
was almost an ID.

00:22:15.126 --> 00:22:17.190
But then, all these algorithms are

00:22:17.191 --> 00:22:19.595
purely for that sort of feature vector case.

00:22:19.596 --> 00:22:23.145
In my weather data, we're again,

00:22:23.146 --> 00:22:24.475
just having to figure out how we can

00:22:24.476 --> 00:22:27.445
engineer the features to take what is very much

00:22:27.446 --> 00:22:30.800
a spatio-temporal time series and turn it into

00:22:30.801 --> 00:22:32.255
little database views that

00:22:32.256 --> 00:22:36.155
are approximately exchangeable.

00:22:40.605 --> 00:22:43.775
Okay, well, the second algorithm I want to

00:22:43.776 --> 00:22:47.985
mention is even simpler, is called LODA.

00:22:50.165 --> 00:22:54.725
Given a set of data points, what it does is,

00:22:54.726 --> 00:22:57.175
is create a set of capital M, say again,

00:22:57.176 --> 00:22:59.685
say 100 sparse random projections.

00:22:59.686 --> 00:23:03.125
So, it randomly chooses square root of d,

00:23:03.126 --> 00:23:05.700
where d, is the dimension of the data, square root of d,

00:23:05.701 --> 00:23:08.365
of the dimensions, and then

00:23:08.366 --> 00:23:11.895
defines a random projection onto a one-dimension,

00:23:11.896 --> 00:23:14.850
and then fits a one-dimensional density model.

00:23:14.851 --> 00:23:17.400
I've drawn it as a kernel density estimate,

00:23:17.401 --> 00:23:22.285
but they actually use an adaptive histogram estimator.

00:23:22.286 --> 00:23:25.350
Then what they do, is just calculate

00:23:25.351 --> 00:23:29.630
the average of the minus log density of a query point,

00:23:29.631 --> 00:23:31.910
so the average surprise.

00:23:31.911 --> 00:23:33.785
We can see, if we took a data point

00:23:33.786 --> 00:23:35.535
like this one right here,

00:23:35.536 --> 00:23:36.905
it would have a pretty high

00:23:36.906 --> 00:23:38.265
probability in this projection.

00:23:38.266 --> 00:23:40.640
But of course, if we got some other projection,

00:23:40.641 --> 00:23:44.230
then it will be mapped to a low-density tail.

00:23:44.231 --> 00:23:47.675
So, this is extremely simple idea,

00:23:47.676 --> 00:23:50.905
but also works surprisingly well presumably

00:23:50.906 --> 00:23:52.395
because they're not

00:23:52.396 --> 00:23:55.155
super however interactions in our data.

00:23:55.156 --> 00:23:58.895
So looking at three or four dimensions

00:23:58.896 --> 00:24:01.805
at a time, works pretty well.

00:24:02.385 --> 00:24:06.260
So then, how do we do our analysis?

00:24:06.261 --> 00:24:11.295
I point you to the archive papers for the details.

00:24:11.296 --> 00:24:13.715
But we basically, chose a couple of

00:24:13.716 --> 00:24:16.975
metrics either the area under the ROC curve,

00:24:16.976 --> 00:24:19.590
which we put through a LODA transform,

00:24:19.591 --> 00:24:23.200
or the log of the lift,

00:24:23.201 --> 00:24:24.765
which is related to the area

00:24:24.766 --> 00:24:26.945
under the precision-recall curve.

00:24:26.946 --> 00:24:29.135
Then, just try to set up

00:24:29.136 --> 00:24:33.080
a linear model to see it across all of these benchmarks,

00:24:33.081 --> 00:24:36.825
how much of the variation in the metric could be

00:24:36.826 --> 00:24:40.425
explained by the relative frequency of the anomalies,

00:24:40.426 --> 00:24:41.955
the difficulty of the points,

00:24:41.956 --> 00:24:45.915
the clusteredness, the amount of irrelevant features,

00:24:45.916 --> 00:24:47.375
what we call the mother set,

00:24:47.376 --> 00:24:49.015
the original, the domain

00:24:49.016 --> 00:24:51.455
from which the original dataset was drawn,

00:24:51.456 --> 00:24:53.565
and which algorithm are we using?

00:24:53.566 --> 00:24:56.290
Because I wanted to say, well,

00:24:56.291 --> 00:24:59.440
a typical finding in machine learning is that

00:24:59.441 --> 00:25:01.395
the choice of problem is much

00:25:01.396 --> 00:25:03.155
more important than which algorithm you use,

00:25:03.156 --> 00:25:06.605
and we were curious if that was true here or not.

00:25:06.606 --> 00:25:09.245
The answer is pretty much true also.

00:25:09.246 --> 00:25:13.550
So, here are those five factors.

00:25:13.551 --> 00:25:18.715
Here is the change in the R squared basically,

00:25:18.716 --> 00:25:22.910
how much variation can be explained in these two metrics?

00:25:22.911 --> 00:25:25.300
So area under the ROC curve,

00:25:25.301 --> 00:25:28.150
if we take a model and delete the dataset feature,

00:25:28.151 --> 00:25:31.125
how much R squared do we lose,

00:25:31.126 --> 00:25:33.025
or how much can we assign to the dataset?

00:25:33.026 --> 00:25:37.235
And that's the most important variable

00:25:37.236 --> 00:25:39.860
for explaining the observed AUC.

00:25:39.861 --> 00:25:41.580
Next is a relative frequency.

00:25:41.581 --> 00:25:43.015
So, if you have

00:25:43.016 --> 00:25:45.665
a dataset where the anomalies are very rare,

00:25:45.666 --> 00:25:47.385
we can do very well because

00:25:47.386 --> 00:25:49.210
we have very little contamination.

00:25:49.211 --> 00:25:51.380
As the nominees get more common,

00:25:51.381 --> 00:25:55.040
then these techniques fail.

00:25:55.041 --> 00:25:56.925
Because really only one of them has

00:25:56.926 --> 00:26:01.695
any explicit attempt to be robust and use a robust loss,

00:26:01.696 --> 00:26:03.200
that's this technique called

00:26:03.201 --> 00:26:05.340
robust kernel density estimation.

00:26:05.341 --> 00:26:07.775
All the others are just plunging

00:26:07.776 --> 00:26:11.145
forward fitting some kind of a model.

00:26:11.245 --> 00:26:13.885
So, the choice of algorithm is

00:26:13.886 --> 00:26:15.890
really only the third most important factor,

00:26:15.891 --> 00:26:17.345
and really not a whole lot more

00:26:17.346 --> 00:26:19.730
important than the others.

00:26:19.731 --> 00:26:22.430
But with that caveat,

00:26:22.431 --> 00:26:25.390
here are the different algorithms that we tested,

00:26:25.391 --> 00:26:29.535
sorted by decreasing change

00:26:29.536 --> 00:26:33.425
in the metric compared to a control baseline.

00:26:33.426 --> 00:26:35.065
What we see, is actually,

00:26:35.066 --> 00:26:36.615
the Isolation Forest was performing

00:26:36.616 --> 00:26:38.720
better than the other methods,

00:26:38.721 --> 00:26:40.555
and then there's sort of like a five-way

00:26:40.556 --> 00:26:41.905
tie for second place.

00:26:41.906 --> 00:26:44.815
Surprisingly, the one-class Support Vector Machine

00:26:44.816 --> 00:26:46.465
and Support Vector Data Description really

00:26:46.466 --> 00:26:48.735
performed the worst on

00:26:48.736 --> 00:26:51.150
these problems, which was quite a shock.

00:26:51.151 --> 00:26:52.575
Because I think if you asked

00:26:52.576 --> 00:26:54.185
a random machine learning person,

00:26:54.186 --> 00:26:55.755
"If I had a one-class problem and I

00:26:55.756 --> 00:26:57.865
needed to fit it, what would you use?"

00:26:57.866 --> 00:26:59.395
And they'd all say, "Well, gee,

00:26:59.396 --> 00:27:02.825
how about Scholkopf has this algorithm."

00:27:02.835 --> 00:27:05.390
We're a little puzzled about this.

00:27:05.391 --> 00:27:06.665
Of course, it's possible that we

00:27:06.666 --> 00:27:08.680
just didn't tune these properly.

00:27:08.681 --> 00:27:10.700
Although, we tried a lot of things,

00:27:10.701 --> 00:27:12.865
but we wanted to have a fully

00:27:12.866 --> 00:27:15.500
automated tuning procedure So,

00:27:15.501 --> 00:27:18.575
it may be that we didn't do that well.

00:27:18.576 --> 00:27:20.700
But there have been some papers

00:27:20.701 --> 00:27:22.615
recently in literature and auto tuning those,

00:27:22.616 --> 00:27:26.030
and so we were trying to use the current best practices.

00:27:26.031 --> 00:27:28.305
My own hypothesis here,

00:27:28.306 --> 00:27:33.775
is that the metrics

00:27:33.776 --> 00:27:35.700
that we're evaluating on are really

00:27:35.701 --> 00:27:38.375
related to how well you can rank the data points.

00:27:38.376 --> 00:27:40.705
Whereas these methods really just set it up

00:27:40.706 --> 00:27:44.150
as are you inside or outside of a particular quantile,

00:27:44.151 --> 00:27:46.585
a particular level set of the density.

00:27:46.586 --> 00:27:48.885
So, it's not clear that they're really been trained for

00:27:48.886 --> 00:27:51.850
the right to do well on these metrics.

00:27:51.851 --> 00:27:54.815
So, maybe that's why they don't do as well as some of

00:27:54.816 --> 00:27:59.385
these more distance-based or density-based approaches.

00:28:00.705 --> 00:28:03.965
So, we also find in general that

00:28:03.966 --> 00:28:05.445
the Isolation Forest is a bit more

00:28:05.446 --> 00:28:07.095
robust to irrelevant features,

00:28:07.096 --> 00:28:08.925
and that it's also a bit more

00:28:08.926 --> 00:28:12.415
robust to clustered anomaly points.

00:28:12.416 --> 00:28:14.105
So that may be why it has

00:28:14.106 --> 00:28:17.305
this slight advantage over the other methods.

00:28:17.475 --> 00:28:20.985
Okay. Well, let's move on now to thinking

00:28:20.986 --> 00:28:22.905
about whether we can have

00:28:22.906 --> 00:28:25.215
some kind of a theory to explain what's going on.

00:28:25.216 --> 00:28:26.555
Because one of the things we were quite

00:28:26.556 --> 00:28:28.975
surprised at is when we look at the learning curves,

00:28:28.976 --> 00:28:31.775
these algorithms all learn quite quickly.

00:28:31.776 --> 00:28:34.655
Of course, if we look at

00:28:34.656 --> 00:28:38.225
what existing sample complexity theory do we

00:28:38.226 --> 00:28:42.490
have for this kind of one-class or density estimation,

00:28:42.491 --> 00:28:45.264
it's often quite discouraging.

00:28:45.265 --> 00:28:48.555
So if you look at full density estimation,

00:28:48.556 --> 00:28:50.485
you typically need a number of examples

00:28:50.486 --> 00:28:53.390
that's exponential in the dimension of the data,

00:28:53.391 --> 00:28:57.985
and that doesn't match our experience.

00:28:57.986 --> 00:28:59.865
For the quantile methods,

00:28:59.866 --> 00:29:02.950
there are polynomial sample complexity results.

00:29:02.951 --> 00:29:06.765
So, our observations are

00:29:06.766 --> 00:29:09.850
maybe more consistent with something polynomial time.

00:29:09.851 --> 00:29:13.065
So, my colleague Alan Fern came up with

00:29:13.066 --> 00:29:16.715
this theory we call rare pattern anomaly detection,

00:29:16.716 --> 00:29:18.315
and it works as follows.

00:29:18.316 --> 00:29:19.980
So we imagine we're going to define,

00:29:19.981 --> 00:29:22.115
this is very valiant

00:29:22.116 --> 00:29:24.095
style pattern learning kind of thing,

00:29:24.096 --> 00:29:27.685
we're going to define a space of patterns or regions.

00:29:27.686 --> 00:29:29.685
So we'll define a pattern

00:29:29.686 --> 00:29:32.755
as a Boolean function that takes a point in d,

00:29:32.756 --> 00:29:35.720
dimensional space, and maps it either zero or one.

00:29:35.721 --> 00:29:37.235
So it might be a half plane

00:29:37.236 --> 00:29:39.745
or an axis-parallel hyper-rectangle,

00:29:39.746 --> 00:29:42.450
some kind of region.

00:29:42.451 --> 00:29:44.370
We'll let capital H,

00:29:44.371 --> 00:29:48.570
be the set of all the patterns in our pattern space.

00:29:48.571 --> 00:29:50.915
Then, the idea is to define a notion of

00:29:50.916 --> 00:29:53.540
a rare pattern and a common pattern.

00:29:53.541 --> 00:29:55.925
To do this, we need to do a little bit

00:29:55.926 --> 00:29:59.435
of being careful about measures.

00:29:59.436 --> 00:30:02.790
So we're going to assume some baseline measure.

00:30:02.791 --> 00:30:06.905
So if we're in say a hyper box in d,

00:30:06.906 --> 00:30:08.085
dimensional space, we might use

00:30:08.086 --> 00:30:09.135
just the Lebesgue measure,

00:30:09.136 --> 00:30:10.810
the uniform measure over that.

00:30:10.811 --> 00:30:12.955
But if we're in unbounded real space,

00:30:12.956 --> 00:30:14.855
we need to use something like a standard

00:30:14.856 --> 00:30:18.125
multivariate Gaussian as our baseline measure.

00:30:18.126 --> 00:30:23.390
We'll let mu(h) be the measure of that pattern,

00:30:23.391 --> 00:30:26.275
pattern h. Then, let's let p,

00:30:26.276 --> 00:30:30.030
be the probability density of the nominal data points,

00:30:30.031 --> 00:30:32.810
and it's defined over Rd, also.

00:30:32.811 --> 00:30:35.175
And will let p(h) be the probability

00:30:35.176 --> 00:30:37.945
assigned to pattern h. So,

00:30:37.946 --> 00:30:39.225
we'll say a pattern h,

00:30:39.226 --> 00:30:41.755
is tau-rare if the sort of

00:30:41.756 --> 00:30:43.845
normalized probability density is

00:30:43.846 --> 00:30:46.140
less than or equal to some threshold tau,

00:30:46.141 --> 00:30:50.140
and otherwise we'll say that pattern is tau-common.

00:30:50.141 --> 00:30:53.430
Then our definition of what we want,

00:30:53.431 --> 00:30:55.175
is we're going to say,

00:30:55.176 --> 00:30:56.665
a data point now,

00:30:56.666 --> 00:30:59.585
a query point is tau-rare if there exists

00:30:59.586 --> 00:31:03.425
a tau-rare pattern that that data point belongs to.

00:31:03.426 --> 00:31:06.890
So that data point belongs in pattern h,

00:31:06.891 --> 00:31:08.275
and h, is tau-rare.

00:31:08.276 --> 00:31:10.345
So it means there's some way of looking at the data,

00:31:10.346 --> 00:31:14.895
there's some bin in the data that this query point x,

00:31:14.896 --> 00:31:17.355
belongs to that is very unusual

00:31:17.356 --> 00:31:21.215
under the nominal distribution.

00:31:21.216 --> 00:31:23.275
So, then our goal,

00:31:23.276 --> 00:31:26.615
an ideal anomaly detection algorithm would output all of

00:31:26.616 --> 00:31:30.035
the tau-rare query points in a test set,

00:31:30.036 --> 00:31:31.245
and not output any of the

00:31:31.246 --> 00:31:33.685
tau-common points that would be the idea.

00:31:33.686 --> 00:31:37.760
So, I guess what's

00:31:37.761 --> 00:31:40.165
interesting about this model is that instead

00:31:40.166 --> 00:31:41.835
of trying to estimate

00:31:41.836 --> 00:31:43.805
the entire density or

00:31:43.806 --> 00:31:46.715
even trying to estimate a level set of the density,

00:31:46.716 --> 00:31:49.490
we're just choosing some set of

00:31:49.491 --> 00:31:52.365
regions in the space and just

00:31:52.366 --> 00:31:54.685
trying to figure out roughly what

00:31:54.686 --> 00:31:58.440
their probability density is or the probability of those.

00:31:58.441 --> 00:31:59.745
I think that this is

00:31:59.746 --> 00:32:02.685
why if we don't have too many of those regions,

00:32:02.686 --> 00:32:05.035
then we can get

00:32:05.036 --> 00:32:06.785
uniformly good estimates over most

00:32:06.786 --> 00:32:09.365
of them from a relatively small sample.

00:32:09.366 --> 00:32:12.035
So, we define an algorithm to

00:32:12.036 --> 00:32:14.910
be probably approximately correct RPAD.

00:32:14.911 --> 00:32:18.095
If for any probability density p,

00:32:18.096 --> 00:32:19.795
and any threshold tau,

00:32:19.796 --> 00:32:22.290
it draws a polynomial size sample.

00:32:22.291 --> 00:32:24.360
Then with probability one minus Epsilon,

00:32:24.361 --> 00:32:27.530
it detects all the tau-rare points and rejects,

00:32:27.531 --> 00:32:31.150
does not output any tau plus Epsilon common points.

00:32:31.151 --> 00:32:33.025
So we have to introduce a sort of

00:32:33.026 --> 00:32:34.815
elbow room parameter to

00:32:34.816 --> 00:32:36.755
allow the algorithm to make some mistakes.

00:32:36.756 --> 00:32:39.775
So it's allowed if a point is between

00:32:39.776 --> 00:32:43.225
tau plus Epsilon rare and tau rare,

00:32:43.226 --> 00:32:48.715
then the algorithm can do anything it wants there.

00:32:48.716 --> 00:32:50.165
So, what we end up with

00:32:50.166 --> 00:32:53.625
is an algorithm such as the following.

00:32:53.775 --> 00:32:57.080
We can draw a polynomial size sample,

00:32:57.081 --> 00:32:59.670
and we'll talk about what this is in a moment,

00:32:59.671 --> 00:33:02.285
and then we just estimate p-hat,

00:33:02.286 --> 00:33:04.025
is the fraction of the sample

00:33:04.026 --> 00:33:06.280
that belongs to each of our patterns,

00:33:06.281 --> 00:33:10.280
and then we can calculate the normalized rareness.

00:33:10.281 --> 00:33:12.445
Then we just say a query point

00:33:12.446 --> 00:33:14.445
is declared to be an anomaly

00:33:14.446 --> 00:33:16.750
if one of these tau-rare patterns

00:33:16.751 --> 00:33:19.795
is made true by that query point.

00:33:19.835 --> 00:33:25.125
So we can prove a sample complexity result that

00:33:25.126 --> 00:33:27.535
says that the number of samples we

00:33:27.536 --> 00:33:30.715
need is on the order of one over Epsilon squared,

00:33:30.716 --> 00:33:31.975
the log of the number of

00:33:31.976 --> 00:33:34.175
patterns plus log of one over Delta.

00:33:34.176 --> 00:33:37.490
So, very kind of typical pack result.

00:33:37.491 --> 00:33:39.035
It's a little unfortunate that this

00:33:39.036 --> 00:33:40.905
is Epsilon squared here.

00:33:40.906 --> 00:33:44.875
So that's going to mean if we want Epsilon to be 0.01,

00:33:44.876 --> 00:33:48.295
then we're going to need 10,000.

00:33:48.296 --> 00:33:51.635
This will be a big number, a big constant.

00:33:51.636 --> 00:33:55.440
Then we can get a VC dimension version of this,

00:33:55.441 --> 00:33:57.365
where the VC dimension plugs in

00:33:57.366 --> 00:34:00.375
here times log of one over Epsilon squared.

00:34:00.875 --> 00:34:03.465
So, there are lots of

00:34:03.466 --> 00:34:06.035
different practical algorithms you

00:34:06.036 --> 00:34:07.930
can design with this procedure.

00:34:07.931 --> 00:34:12.740
So, half spaces and axis-aligned hyper-rectangles,

00:34:12.741 --> 00:34:14.015
and in our paper, we worked out

00:34:14.016 --> 00:34:16.060
the VC dimension of all of these.

00:34:16.061 --> 00:34:18.175
In particular, we designed a version of

00:34:18.176 --> 00:34:22.160
the Isolation Forest that exactly matches this theory.

00:34:22.161 --> 00:34:25.285
To do that, the pattern space

00:34:25.286 --> 00:34:27.785
has to be chosen a priori in this theory,

00:34:27.786 --> 00:34:29.935
it can't be adaptive to the data.

00:34:29.936 --> 00:34:32.745
So to deal with that, we use the usual trick of say,

00:34:32.746 --> 00:34:34.825
taking our training data and dividing it in half,

00:34:34.826 --> 00:34:36.915
and using half the data to grow

00:34:36.916 --> 00:34:41.675
an isolation tree to some fixed depth k,

00:34:41.895 --> 00:34:44.435
that fixes our pattern space,

00:34:44.436 --> 00:34:45.985
and then the second-half of the data,

00:34:45.986 --> 00:34:51.225
we can estimate the probability of all of these bins.

00:34:51.585 --> 00:34:56.505
So, here are some examples on some datasets,

00:34:56.506 --> 00:35:00.435
where the plot on the left is for

00:35:00.436 --> 00:35:02.825
the actual Isolation Forest algorithm

00:35:02.826 --> 00:35:04.445
grown to a fixed depth

00:35:04.446 --> 00:35:08.425
k. This is our theoretical version of

00:35:08.426 --> 00:35:10.435
it also at depth

00:35:10.436 --> 00:35:13.245
k. The vertical axis is the area under the ROC curve,

00:35:13.246 --> 00:35:14.925
and I apologize that the axes

00:35:14.926 --> 00:35:16.550
are different in the two plots.

00:35:16.551 --> 00:35:18.115
Then these different lines are

00:35:18.116 --> 00:35:19.800
for different depths of tree.

00:35:19.801 --> 00:35:21.610
But if we just look in this case,

00:35:21.611 --> 00:35:24.300
the depth one tree is actually works the best.

00:35:24.301 --> 00:35:26.685
We see that in this case,

00:35:26.686 --> 00:35:28.005
the pattern min method

00:35:28.006 --> 00:35:29.855
actually does a little bit better than

00:35:29.856 --> 00:35:34.420
the Isolation Forest on the area under the ROC curve.

00:35:34.421 --> 00:35:37.465
For another dataset particle,

00:35:37.466 --> 00:35:39.420
the Isolation Forest does much better

00:35:39.421 --> 00:35:42.330
because notice this is 0.71 down to 0.66,

00:35:42.331 --> 00:35:44.795
and this would be 0.66 down to 0.52.

00:35:44.796 --> 00:35:47.755
So, all of these curves basically are below all of these,

00:35:47.756 --> 00:35:49.855
so the Isolation Forest was much better.

00:35:49.856 --> 00:35:52.930
Then, here's a dataset shuttle,

00:35:52.931 --> 00:35:55.475
where the RPAD,

00:35:55.476 --> 00:35:57.885
the theoretically inspired algorithm is actually

00:35:57.886 --> 00:36:00.085
doing quite a bit better than Isolation Forest.

00:36:00.086 --> 00:36:04.240
This time, I did get the axes aligned.

00:36:04.241 --> 00:36:07.175
So I think qualitatively, well,

00:36:07.176 --> 00:36:10.605
the shapes of the curves aren't always the same,

00:36:11.585 --> 00:36:14.535
but yeah,

00:36:14.536 --> 00:36:16.515
there's sometimes some funny business

00:36:16.516 --> 00:36:18.325
going on with very small samples.

00:36:18.326 --> 00:36:20.035
This is particularly weird

00:36:20.036 --> 00:36:23.360
that we do very well with very small samples,

00:36:23.361 --> 00:36:26.095
and there's an interesting phenomenon

00:36:26.096 --> 00:36:28.290
going on here that we don't fully understand.

00:36:28.291 --> 00:36:32.815
But, when you have a training sample,

00:36:32.816 --> 00:36:35.784
and it includes some amount of contamination.

00:36:35.785 --> 00:36:38.925
A standard practice that's done in

00:36:38.926 --> 00:36:40.340
the Isolation Forest is to

00:36:40.341 --> 00:36:42.555
train on actually subsets of the data,

00:36:42.556 --> 00:36:44.210
not on the entire dataset,

00:36:44.211 --> 00:36:47.240
and the idea is that you're kind of thinning out.

00:36:47.241 --> 00:36:49.130
It's a robust stratification strategy.

00:36:49.131 --> 00:36:53.135
By training on smaller sub-samples,

00:36:53.136 --> 00:36:58.550
the absolute number of contaminating points is reduced.

00:36:58.551 --> 00:37:00.045
The fraction isn't reduced,

00:37:00.046 --> 00:37:03.695
but it seems that if you have a tight clustered points,

00:37:03.696 --> 00:37:06.225
you're reducing that local density of that cluster.

00:37:06.226 --> 00:37:10.565
And I think this helps the algorithms do a better job,

00:37:10.566 --> 00:37:11.135
but we don't have

00:37:11.136 --> 00:37:12.970
a good theoretical understanding of this.

00:37:12.971 --> 00:37:14.780
But empirically,

00:37:14.781 --> 00:37:16.445
even the original authors of

00:37:16.446 --> 00:37:17.765
the Isolation Forest algorithm

00:37:17.766 --> 00:37:20.065
say that you should train on

00:37:20.066 --> 00:37:21.815
a small subsets of

00:37:21.816 --> 00:37:23.490
the data rather than the entire dataset,

00:37:23.491 --> 00:37:25.355
and that this often helps.

00:37:25.356 --> 00:37:27.715
For those of us in machine learning it's like,

00:37:27.716 --> 00:37:30.045
"What, you're not using all your data?"

00:37:30.046 --> 00:37:31.585
But of course, we're building an ensemble.

00:37:31.586 --> 00:37:33.530
So across the whole ensemble,

00:37:33.531 --> 00:37:35.430
we're using all of our data.

00:37:35.431 --> 00:37:36.815
The smaller the subsets,

00:37:36.816 --> 00:37:38.440
the bigger the ensemble needs to be,

00:37:38.441 --> 00:37:40.845
because you also have to reduce

00:37:40.846 --> 00:37:42.085
the variance that you've introduced

00:37:42.086 --> 00:37:44.325
by choosing these random subsets.

00:37:44.326 --> 00:37:48.385
So this is a paradox that where this kind of thing,

00:37:48.386 --> 00:37:51.355
where the small samples are actually performing well.

00:37:51.356 --> 00:37:53.085
It's not observed all the time,

00:37:53.086 --> 00:37:54.635
but it's observed enough of the time

00:37:54.636 --> 00:37:56.575
that we think it's a real phenomenon

00:37:56.576 --> 00:37:58.015
and we're trying to

00:37:58.016 --> 00:38:01.485
find a nice theoretical understanding of it.

00:38:02.165 --> 00:38:06.735
Okay. So the conclusions from this is that we feel that

00:38:06.736 --> 00:38:09.920
this rare pattern anomaly detection theory

00:38:09.921 --> 00:38:11.455
captures a lot of the behavior of

00:38:11.456 --> 00:38:14.505
the Isolation Forest or of LODA,

00:38:14.506 --> 00:38:18.925
because these methods are solving a simpler problem than

00:38:18.926 --> 00:38:20.765
full density estimation or

00:38:20.766 --> 00:38:24.085
even estimating level sets of the density.

00:38:24.885 --> 00:38:29.185
So, I guess maybe I should pause and say,

00:38:29.186 --> 00:38:31.580
are there any questions about that? Yes.

00:38:31.581 --> 00:38:34.525
&gt;&gt; How does this algorithm compared on

00:38:34.526 --> 00:38:38.365
a high-dimensional data, very high-dimensional data?

00:38:38.366 --> 00:38:42.125
&gt;&gt; Well, I would guess on very high-dimensional data,

00:38:42.126 --> 00:38:47.455
you need to use a very large ensemble of trees.

00:38:49.115 --> 00:38:54.915
Let's see, what's the right way to talk about this?

00:38:54.916 --> 00:39:02.075
If your space of rare patterns is, in some sense,

00:39:02.076 --> 00:39:05.835
if you have a distribution of the data,

00:39:05.836 --> 00:39:07.965
you want to have these rare patterns kind

00:39:07.966 --> 00:39:10.745
of surrounding the data.

00:39:10.746 --> 00:39:13.725
Obviously, one intuition is in high-dimensional space,

00:39:13.726 --> 00:39:15.235
you're going to need a lot of

00:39:15.236 --> 00:39:18.680
those patterns surrounding the cloud of data.

00:39:18.681 --> 00:39:22.180
So, the number of patterns you might need to really tile

00:39:22.181 --> 00:39:27.410
the entire region might grow exponentially.

00:39:27.411 --> 00:39:31.225
So, our result says

00:39:31.226 --> 00:39:34.635
the sample size is polynomial in the number of patterns,

00:39:34.636 --> 00:39:36.095
but the number of patterns that

00:39:36.096 --> 00:39:37.660
you need might be exponential.

00:39:37.661 --> 00:39:39.565
So, that's a good question.

00:39:39.566 --> 00:39:41.385
&gt;&gt; Have you tried this on a bag-of-words?

00:39:41.386 --> 00:39:43.335
&gt;&gt; We have not tried this on

00:39:43.336 --> 00:39:46.080
a bag-of-words thing for example, yeah.

00:39:46.081 --> 00:39:47.955
Now, one might hope,

00:39:47.956 --> 00:39:51.195
so I'll do the standard machine learning thing and say,

00:39:51.196 --> 00:39:52.385
"Oh, but the data really lives

00:39:52.386 --> 00:39:53.910
in a low-dimensional manifold."

00:39:53.911 --> 00:39:55.560
So all we have to do,

00:39:55.561 --> 00:39:58.120
is identify that manifold then.

00:39:58.121 --> 00:39:59.515
Well, but we know that

00:39:59.516 --> 00:40:00.875
tree-type algorithms will in

00:40:00.876 --> 00:40:03.330
some sense follow that manifold.

00:40:03.331 --> 00:40:08.605
So, we would maybe we'll still do okay.

00:40:08.606 --> 00:40:13.370
I'm waving my hands. Yes.

00:40:13.371 --> 00:40:15.005
&gt;&gt; So, it seems like it still

00:40:15.006 --> 00:40:17.860
requires knowing the pattern space.

00:40:17.861 --> 00:40:20.555
But then, if you're dealing with anomaly detection,

00:40:20.556 --> 00:40:24.415
many successful intrusions happen exactly

00:40:24.416 --> 00:40:26.305
because there is some sort of

00:40:26.306 --> 00:40:29.035
behavioral pattern that you didn't take into account,

00:40:29.036 --> 00:40:33.135
and so the adversary was able to exploit it.

00:40:33.136 --> 00:40:34.715
So, is there a way to

00:40:34.716 --> 00:40:37.185
extend or fix this theory to maybe at

00:40:37.186 --> 00:40:39.015
least give you some indication that there's something in

00:40:39.016 --> 00:40:41.565
the data for which you don't maybe have a pattern,

00:40:41.566 --> 00:40:44.885
or you didn't miss the pattern that may be an anomaly?

00:40:44.886 --> 00:40:47.035
&gt;&gt; Well, if we think about what,

00:40:47.036 --> 00:40:48.870
for instance, the Isolation Forest does,

00:40:48.871 --> 00:40:50.430
it actually partitions the entire

00:40:50.431 --> 00:40:52.765
input space into patterns.

00:40:52.766 --> 00:40:54.085
So, there is always a pattern

00:40:54.086 --> 00:40:55.830
for every possible observation.

00:40:55.831 --> 00:40:58.420
Many of those patterns will be essentially empty.

00:40:58.421 --> 00:41:00.665
In fact, I skipped over

00:41:00.666 --> 00:41:02.955
a little detail of the Isolation Forest.

00:41:02.956 --> 00:41:07.070
But when you do a split at a node and you split the data,

00:41:07.071 --> 00:41:13.705
there is little empty spaces

00:41:13.706 --> 00:41:16.275
between the observed range of the data

00:41:16.276 --> 00:41:19.730
in the children and the observed range in the parent.

00:41:19.731 --> 00:41:22.775
They're actually, a query point can fall into

00:41:22.776 --> 00:41:28.180
that empty space in between the observed ranges.

00:41:28.181 --> 00:41:30.725
I'm being very inarticulate here about this.

00:41:30.726 --> 00:41:32.925
But if I have a set

00:41:32.926 --> 00:41:34.255
of data points along

00:41:34.256 --> 00:41:36.750
a line and I choose an arbitrary split,

00:41:36.751 --> 00:41:38.600
there's going to be some space

00:41:38.601 --> 00:41:41.025
between adjacent instances and

00:41:41.026 --> 00:41:43.265
that empty space effect for the algorithm

00:41:43.266 --> 00:41:47.040
allocates two additional patterns for that empty space.

00:41:47.041 --> 00:41:48.385
Because if a query point comes down,

00:41:48.386 --> 00:41:49.575
it's considered to be isolated if

00:41:49.576 --> 00:41:51.275
it falls into this space,

00:41:51.276 --> 00:41:52.515
it was inside the range of

00:41:52.516 --> 00:41:55.050
the parent and outside the range of the child.

00:41:55.051 --> 00:41:58.125
That turns out to be really important.

00:41:58.126 --> 00:41:59.940
If you don't do this right,

00:41:59.941 --> 00:42:01.065
then the performance of

00:42:01.066 --> 00:42:03.265
the algorithm suffers quite a lot.

00:42:03.266 --> 00:42:06.815
So, I guess my first point would be that if we

00:42:06.816 --> 00:42:10.055
use some of the data to build the tree structure,

00:42:10.056 --> 00:42:11.545
and if the tree structure is really

00:42:11.546 --> 00:42:12.755
following the structure of the data,

00:42:12.756 --> 00:42:14.435
then we will have tiled the space nicely.

00:42:14.436 --> 00:42:17.790
And every leaf node by construction is going to

00:42:17.791 --> 00:42:21.990
be rare because it will have at most one point in it.

00:42:21.991 --> 00:42:23.865
So we're going to have a lot of reasons that

00:42:23.866 --> 00:42:25.965
have essentially zero points or one in them,

00:42:25.966 --> 00:42:28.335
and we've also completely tiled the space.

00:42:28.336 --> 00:42:31.915
So, if there is an unusual behavior,

00:42:31.935 --> 00:42:34.125
it will fall into one of

00:42:34.126 --> 00:42:38.105
these empty spaces basically and will be detected.

00:42:38.445 --> 00:42:41.290
&gt;&gt; Then how do you tell between

00:42:41.291 --> 00:42:43.335
interesting anomalous behaviors

00:42:43.336 --> 00:42:44.740
and uninteresting anomalous behaviors?

00:42:44.741 --> 00:42:46.265
Because essentially, you won't be able to do that.

00:42:46.266 --> 00:42:47.530
&gt;&gt; Well, yeah, because we're only

00:42:47.531 --> 00:42:49.405
doing outlier detection here,

00:42:49.406 --> 00:42:51.690
and we have no knowledge that an outlier.

00:42:51.691 --> 00:42:53.345
We can't tell the difference between

00:42:53.346 --> 00:42:55.235
an outlier that's part of the natural variation of

00:42:55.236 --> 00:42:57.895
the distribution and an outlier that's due to

00:42:57.896 --> 00:43:01.790
some unusual behavior that's

00:43:01.791 --> 00:43:03.725
caused by something different like

00:43:03.726 --> 00:43:05.800
a fraudster or something, yeah.

00:43:05.801 --> 00:43:07.840
In fact, of course,

00:43:07.841 --> 00:43:09.155
a fraudster is going to try to

00:43:09.156 --> 00:43:12.555
mimic the nominal distribution,

00:43:12.556 --> 00:43:16.095
and so we might want to try to

00:43:16.096 --> 00:43:22.015
use some essential behavior,

00:43:22.016 --> 00:43:24.845
things that the fraudulent user is not

00:43:24.846 --> 00:43:28.485
able to manipulate to help us detect their behavior.

00:43:28.486 --> 00:43:31.105
But, all I'll say is that

00:43:31.106 --> 00:43:34.715
in the competitions inside this DARPA program,

00:43:34.716 --> 00:43:36.625
we were number one in several months

00:43:36.626 --> 00:43:38.395
using these kind of techniques.

00:43:38.396 --> 00:43:40.635
So, maybe that's a tribute

00:43:40.636 --> 00:43:42.005
to our future engineering skills

00:43:42.006 --> 00:43:46.490
or maybe the red team made it too easy, I don't know.

00:43:46.491 --> 00:43:48.930
But these methods were working pretty well.

00:43:48.931 --> 00:43:51.115
We're competing against an IBM team that

00:43:51.116 --> 00:43:54.855
actually has a product in this space, so I don't know.

00:43:58.545 --> 00:44:01.615
Anything else? Okay, well,

00:44:01.616 --> 00:44:03.045
let me move on now to

00:44:03.046 --> 00:44:05.445
assuming that we can find these anomalies,

00:44:05.446 --> 00:44:07.185
how can we explain them to the user?

00:44:07.186 --> 00:44:13.375
So, our basic idea is the following.

00:44:13.376 --> 00:44:16.145
We were really troubled

00:44:16.146 --> 00:44:18.325
by what constitutes a good explanation,

00:44:18.326 --> 00:44:20.555
and how could we measure that in

00:44:20.556 --> 00:44:24.930
a rigorous way without doing human subject studies.

00:44:24.931 --> 00:44:28.575
So, here is the thing that we came up with.

00:44:28.576 --> 00:44:30.455
We imagined that the goal of

00:44:30.456 --> 00:44:33.135
our anomaly detection system is to

00:44:33.136 --> 00:44:34.375
persuade the analyst that

00:44:34.376 --> 00:44:36.995
a candidate anomaly is a real anomaly.

00:44:36.996 --> 00:44:39.215
The idea is we're going to expose

00:44:39.216 --> 00:44:42.115
one feature at a time to the analyst,

00:44:42.225 --> 00:44:45.495
and in the worst case

00:44:45.496 --> 00:44:46.715
until we've presented all of

00:44:46.716 --> 00:44:48.665
the different features to the analyst.

00:44:48.666 --> 00:44:52.235
Then with an appropriate visualization tool,

00:44:52.236 --> 00:44:54.985
until we've either convinced the analyst that it

00:44:54.986 --> 00:44:57.415
really is an anomaly or the analyst says, no, it isn't.

00:44:57.416 --> 00:44:59.150
We sort of view the analyst,

00:44:59.151 --> 00:45:01.865
their job is really to decide whether to say launch

00:45:01.866 --> 00:45:03.425
an internal investigation like

00:45:03.426 --> 00:45:06.520
a fraud investigation, or not.

00:45:06.521 --> 00:45:11.505
So, this is the the kind of model here.

00:45:11.506 --> 00:45:14.225
So, we call this a sequential feature explanation.

00:45:14.226 --> 00:45:20.185
We had a paper at UAI two years ago on this.

00:45:20.186 --> 00:45:24.145
So in pictures, the vertical axis here is the experts or

00:45:24.146 --> 00:45:26.620
the analysts' assessment of

00:45:26.621 --> 00:45:30.400
the probability that the data point is a nominal,

00:45:30.401 --> 00:45:34.580
not an anomaly, given the features you've seen so far.

00:45:34.581 --> 00:45:36.905
So we'll say that notionally it starts out pretty

00:45:36.906 --> 00:45:39.585
close to one because say,

00:45:39.586 --> 00:45:41.800
one minus Epsilon,

00:45:41.801 --> 00:45:43.715
where Epsilon is the background

00:45:43.716 --> 00:45:45.515
probability of being an anomaly.

00:45:45.516 --> 00:45:48.190
In our sequential feature explanation

00:45:48.191 --> 00:45:49.915
is some permutation of the feature,

00:45:49.916 --> 00:45:50.915
say, feature number three,

00:45:50.916 --> 00:45:52.250
we think is the most important.

00:45:52.251 --> 00:45:55.060
So we start presenting these features to the expert.

00:45:55.061 --> 00:45:57.510
If we're being persuasive,

00:45:57.511 --> 00:45:59.765
the experts posterior belief that

00:45:59.766 --> 00:46:02.410
this is a normal point starts to drop,

00:46:02.411 --> 00:46:04.495
and hopefully goes to zero.

00:46:04.496 --> 00:46:07.370
What we do is choose some threshold,

00:46:07.371 --> 00:46:14.245
and we talk about

00:46:14.246 --> 00:46:15.405
our metric is something we

00:46:15.406 --> 00:46:17.999
call the minimum feature prefix,

00:46:18.000 --> 00:46:21.685
MFP, which in this case will be four.

00:46:21.686 --> 00:46:25.105
It took us the first four features in our ordering,

00:46:25.106 --> 00:46:27.240
we're required to drive

00:46:27.241 --> 00:46:29.605
the expert's assessment down below say point,

00:46:29.606 --> 00:46:31.955
whatever that is, 0.25.

00:46:31.956 --> 00:46:37.455
So, this is the MFP is our metric.

00:46:37.456 --> 00:46:40.140
Across the whole dataset of anomalies,

00:46:40.141 --> 00:46:41.585
we'll look at the average of

00:46:41.586 --> 00:46:43.925
that as being our performance metric.

00:46:43.926 --> 00:46:49.355
So far, anomaly detectors working well.

00:46:50.025 --> 00:46:53.475
Or if we have a good explanation algorithm,

00:46:53.476 --> 00:46:56.165
it should use short explanations.

00:46:56.166 --> 00:46:58.205
So this is sort of

00:46:58.206 --> 00:47:00.575
the minimum kind of thing we might have and obviously,

00:47:00.576 --> 00:47:02.630
it's only appropriate for

00:47:02.631 --> 00:47:05.985
problems where the features are meaningful.

00:47:08.335 --> 00:47:11.535
So, in this case, our technique,

00:47:11.536 --> 00:47:14.785
we relied on another one of our anomaly detectors,

00:47:14.786 --> 00:47:17.085
which is an ensemble of Gaussian mixture model,

00:47:17.086 --> 00:47:19.260
so the EGMM algorithm.

00:47:19.261 --> 00:47:21.695
We had originally developed this just as a control.

00:47:21.696 --> 00:47:26.165
So we apply PCA and then in the PCA space,

00:47:26.166 --> 00:47:30.065
we fit an ensemble of Gaussian mixture models,

00:47:30.066 --> 00:47:33.120
where we randomly vary the number

00:47:33.121 --> 00:47:36.840
of mixture components and the starting initialization,

00:47:36.841 --> 00:47:40.145
and we discard mixture components

00:47:40.146 --> 00:47:42.315
that didn't fit the data very well.

00:47:42.316 --> 00:47:44.710
And of the ones that survive,

00:47:44.711 --> 00:47:53.750
then we can get our anomaly scores are actually,

00:47:53.751 --> 00:47:56.525
again, the average surprise across those models.

00:47:56.526 --> 00:48:00.315
So it's the product of the density estimates I guess.

00:48:01.585 --> 00:48:04.245
So, then, we use a simple greedy algorithm.

00:48:04.246 --> 00:48:08.920
So, the score of a particular feature

00:48:08.921 --> 00:48:13.185
is how much the log likelihood

00:48:13.186 --> 00:48:15.205
drops when we marginalize out,

00:48:15.206 --> 00:48:16.335
or I've got this backwards there

00:48:16.336 --> 00:48:17.625
should probably a minus sign here.

00:48:17.626 --> 00:48:21.220
It's how much the surprise decreases when we

00:48:21.221 --> 00:48:23.615
compare the marginalized distribution

00:48:23.616 --> 00:48:26.165
to the original distribution, so right there.

00:48:26.166 --> 00:48:28.405
This will always have

00:48:28.406 --> 00:48:30.195
a higher probability density after

00:48:30.196 --> 00:48:32.475
we've marginalized that one feature,

00:48:32.476 --> 00:48:35.520
then the full distribution had.

00:48:35.521 --> 00:48:38.445
So, we do this, we score each feature and

00:48:38.446 --> 00:48:41.595
the one that led to the biggest change in the density,

00:48:41.596 --> 00:48:43.890
we say that's the feature of the biggest impact,

00:48:43.891 --> 00:48:46.575
and then we remove it from

00:48:46.576 --> 00:48:49.985
this set of candidate features and add it

00:48:49.986 --> 00:48:54.075
to our answer and just do that in a greedy fashion.

00:48:55.595 --> 00:48:59.729
Again, we used a bunch of our datasets.

00:48:59.730 --> 00:49:01.445
We only used seven of

00:49:01.446 --> 00:49:06.440
the UCI domains instead of 19 in this study.

00:49:06.441 --> 00:49:09.415
Instead of using real human analysts,

00:49:09.416 --> 00:49:12.265
we trained regularized random forests

00:49:12.266 --> 00:49:13.875
on the supervised version of the data,

00:49:13.876 --> 00:49:17.835
so they can see all the data.

00:49:17.836 --> 00:49:20.360
Then the question was,

00:49:20.361 --> 00:49:23.025
we would convince them if they

00:49:23.026 --> 00:49:27.424
saw all the first four features,

00:49:27.425 --> 00:49:29.505
is there a predicted probability

00:49:29.506 --> 00:49:31.695
that it belongs to the anomaly class,

00:49:31.696 --> 00:49:33.665
or to the nominal class

00:49:33.666 --> 00:49:36.115
as it dropped below some threshold.

00:49:36.116 --> 00:49:42.795
So, we compared against a random ordering of

00:49:42.796 --> 00:49:45.095
the features and against an Oracle that

00:49:45.096 --> 00:49:49.060
actually knows the labels of the data.

00:49:49.061 --> 00:49:52.530
What we saw was that we always did better than random,

00:49:52.531 --> 00:49:55.735
and sometimes we do almost as well as the Oracle.

00:49:55.736 --> 00:49:57.765
So, this is random ordering.

00:49:57.766 --> 00:49:59.075
This is the Oracle in blue,

00:49:59.076 --> 00:50:02.055
and this is our greedy algorithm.

00:50:02.265 --> 00:50:04.805
Sometimes, we don't do as well.

00:50:04.806 --> 00:50:06.155
I guess one thing that's kind of

00:50:06.156 --> 00:50:07.445
interesting is that for most of

00:50:07.446 --> 00:50:10.365
these Irvine type domains, on average,

00:50:10.366 --> 00:50:13.085
the Oracle only needs to show the analyst one

00:50:13.086 --> 00:50:16.195
or at most two features to give an explanation.

00:50:16.196 --> 00:50:18.750
So, that's convincing.

00:50:18.751 --> 00:50:21.125
So, that's kind of interesting.

00:50:21.126 --> 00:50:24.825
This gives us a kind of metric of how hard a dataset is.

00:50:24.826 --> 00:50:27.485
So, if we look at the KD1999 dataset,

00:50:27.486 --> 00:50:29.125
for instance, it's always

00:50:29.126 --> 00:50:31.055
the case that you need only one feature.

00:50:31.056 --> 00:50:34.100
Even our algorithm only needs to

00:50:34.101 --> 00:50:37.905
use one feature to explain an anomaly.

00:50:37.906 --> 00:50:39.350
Whereas if we look at our

00:50:39.351 --> 00:50:43.170
this Adam's insider threat database,

00:50:43.171 --> 00:50:44.615
you need about seven on average,

00:50:44.616 --> 00:50:47.405
so it's a much more challenging problem.

00:50:47.935 --> 00:50:50.915
Well, if we're going to be talking to

00:50:50.916 --> 00:50:54.245
an analyst and convincing them,

00:50:54.246 --> 00:50:56.405
we can also get feedback from an analyst.

00:50:56.406 --> 00:50:59.045
So, we wanted to also then look at,

00:50:59.046 --> 00:51:00.685
could we take the feedback

00:51:00.686 --> 00:51:02.015
from the analyst and feed it back into

00:51:02.016 --> 00:51:04.500
our anomaly detection process to

00:51:04.501 --> 00:51:07.435
re-rank the candidates that we have.

00:51:07.436 --> 00:51:11.505
So, we had a paper at ICDM a year and a half

00:51:11.506 --> 00:51:16.260
ago showing that this could be a potentially big win.

00:51:16.261 --> 00:51:18.980
So what we do is,

00:51:18.981 --> 00:51:23.245
we require one parameter which is a quantile,

00:51:23.246 --> 00:51:26.435
basically corresponds to how many cases

00:51:26.436 --> 00:51:29.160
can an analyst look at in a given,

00:51:29.161 --> 00:51:30.735
like in a day or a month,

00:51:30.736 --> 00:51:33.875
whatever the time period is that we're analyzing.

00:51:33.876 --> 00:51:37.025
So, the idea is that the analyst can look and say,

00:51:37.026 --> 00:51:40.160
Sixty cases in a month.

00:51:40.161 --> 00:51:43.630
We want to optimize the 60 cases we present."

00:51:43.631 --> 00:51:47.145
So we start by presenting to

00:51:47.146 --> 00:51:49.165
the analyst the highest scoring anomaly

00:51:49.166 --> 00:51:51.235
we have and then the analyst says,

00:51:51.236 --> 00:51:53.055
"Yes, that's the case I'm going

00:51:53.056 --> 00:51:55.175
to open an investigation, or no, it isn't."

00:51:55.176 --> 00:51:56.980
And then we feed back that.

00:51:56.981 --> 00:51:58.415
And what we've tried to do,

00:51:58.416 --> 00:52:00.040
is learn to reweight.

00:52:00.041 --> 00:52:01.415
In this case, we're working with

00:52:01.416 --> 00:52:03.210
the LODA anomaly detector,

00:52:03.211 --> 00:52:04.875
which remember, creates a bunch of

00:52:04.876 --> 00:52:08.155
random projections, one-dimensional random projections.

00:52:08.156 --> 00:52:11.125
So we learned to reweight those projections to try

00:52:11.126 --> 00:52:15.490
to rank all of the known anomalies above quantile tau,

00:52:15.491 --> 00:52:18.640
and all of the known nominals below quantile tau.

00:52:18.641 --> 00:52:21.055
To do this, we used a modification of

00:52:21.056 --> 00:52:23.825
a method from Stephen Boyd and

00:52:23.826 --> 00:52:27.075
famous colleagues called Accuracy at the Top that

00:52:27.076 --> 00:52:30.595
was published at NIPS several years ago now.

00:52:30.596 --> 00:52:34.155
So, we're re-ranking based on feedback from the expert,

00:52:34.156 --> 00:52:38.955
and we call this active anomaly detection.

00:52:41.055 --> 00:52:43.655
I guess the only interesting thing is

00:52:43.656 --> 00:52:47.135
that if the anomalies are fairly rare,

00:52:47.136 --> 00:52:49.595
then we will have a significant false alarm rate.

00:52:49.596 --> 00:52:51.645
So it could very well be that the first, three, or four,

00:52:51.646 --> 00:52:54.440
five, ten cases we showed to the analyst,

00:52:54.441 --> 00:52:59.595
none of them are actual fraud cases. What can we do?

00:52:59.596 --> 00:53:02.215
Well, one of the advantages of having

00:53:02.216 --> 00:53:03.630
this threshold tau is that

00:53:03.631 --> 00:53:06.125
even with no positive examples,

00:53:06.126 --> 00:53:07.925
we still learn to push down all of

00:53:07.926 --> 00:53:11.175
those non-anomalies below the threshold tau.

00:53:11.176 --> 00:53:13.435
So, this seems to help us learn

00:53:13.436 --> 00:53:15.715
even without any positives,

00:53:15.716 --> 00:53:19.415
but we had to make some minor modifications.

00:53:19.416 --> 00:53:21.205
So, we compared against

00:53:21.206 --> 00:53:23.025
a baseline where we do no learning,

00:53:23.026 --> 00:53:24.705
we just take our initial anomaly scores

00:53:24.706 --> 00:53:26.250
and present them in that order.

00:53:26.251 --> 00:53:28.275
We looked at randomly ordering them,

00:53:28.276 --> 00:53:30.245
and then this is our method.

00:53:30.246 --> 00:53:32.385
Then there are two other methods in the literature.

00:53:32.386 --> 00:53:34.955
One called AI2, which I don't think has

00:53:34.956 --> 00:53:38.160
ever been published except as a CSAIL Tech Report.

00:53:38.161 --> 00:53:41.340
So it's probably a class project or something,

00:53:41.341 --> 00:53:43.410
although it works quite well in some cases.

00:53:43.411 --> 00:53:46.415
Then there's something called SSAD,

00:53:46.416 --> 00:53:48.875
which was in a JAIR paper.

00:53:48.876 --> 00:53:52.105
Their method from Gornitz, et al.

00:53:52.106 --> 00:53:55.905
is called Marginalize and Cluster, I think.

00:53:55.906 --> 00:53:58.175
We thought we had an idea for

00:53:58.176 --> 00:54:00.415
improving it using our Accuracy at the Top method,

00:54:00.416 --> 00:54:03.325
but as you'll see it was not an improvement,

00:54:03.326 --> 00:54:05.320
but just for completeness.

00:54:05.321 --> 00:54:07.285
So, what we're going to plot in these plots,

00:54:07.286 --> 00:54:08.945
is along the horizontal axis is

00:54:08.946 --> 00:54:12.115
how many cases have we presented to the analyst,

00:54:12.116 --> 00:54:13.955
and the vertical axis is how many

00:54:13.956 --> 00:54:15.850
of them turned out to be true anomalies.

00:54:15.851 --> 00:54:17.165
Again, all this is done with

00:54:17.166 --> 00:54:20.505
a simulated analyst, and so on.

00:54:20.506 --> 00:54:22.095
So, we can see that our baseline method

00:54:22.096 --> 00:54:23.490
is this blue curve.

00:54:23.491 --> 00:54:26.985
So, if we have a budget of 60 cases we can look at,

00:54:26.986 --> 00:54:28.305
we find about, I don't know,

00:54:28.306 --> 00:54:30.320
25 or 26 or something.

00:54:30.321 --> 00:54:34.875
Whereas if we can incorporate our feedback, we get 45.

00:54:34.876 --> 00:54:39.210
So, huge advantage from having the analyst in loop.

00:54:39.211 --> 00:54:41.945
However, this is the KD1999 dataset,

00:54:41.946 --> 00:54:43.185
which is extremely easy.

00:54:43.186 --> 00:54:45.445
So let's move onto some other ones.

00:54:45.446 --> 00:54:48.045
So this is a Abalone dataset.

00:54:48.046 --> 00:54:51.465
Here, early on, we

00:54:51.466 --> 00:54:55.705
get some benefit of our method over our baseline.

00:54:56.615 --> 00:54:59.470
No feedback is the blue curve,

00:54:59.471 --> 00:55:00.980
and ours is the red curve.

00:55:00.981 --> 00:55:03.575
Then this is SSAD method,

00:55:03.576 --> 00:55:07.020
which is doing just about as well as ours.

00:55:07.021 --> 00:55:09.425
But if we say, well, suppose we only

00:55:09.426 --> 00:55:11.180
had time to look at 20 cases,

00:55:11.181 --> 00:55:12.775
then we'd still be seeing

00:55:12.776 --> 00:55:15.405
more than a factor of two improvement.

00:55:15.406 --> 00:55:17.525
Eventually, obviously if you go out far

00:55:17.526 --> 00:55:19.335
enough on this, there will be no benefit.

00:55:19.336 --> 00:55:20.855
Because eventually, we'll have

00:55:20.856 --> 00:55:23.180
shown everything to the analyst.

00:55:23.181 --> 00:55:26.500
Here's another one for thyroid.

00:55:26.501 --> 00:55:29.225
In this case, again,

00:55:29.226 --> 00:55:31.565
compared to our baseline and we're doing pretty well.

00:55:31.566 --> 00:55:33.015
In this case, this AI2 method is

00:55:33.016 --> 00:55:35.160
doing almost as well as we are.

00:55:35.161 --> 00:55:37.995
But again, the benefit is potentially almost a factor of

00:55:37.996 --> 00:55:40.700
two in terms of the number of anomalies we can detect.

00:55:40.701 --> 00:55:43.830
In mammography, similar story.

00:55:43.831 --> 00:55:46.305
So in general, for all of these,

00:55:46.306 --> 00:55:48.045
we're seeing that incorporating

00:55:48.046 --> 00:55:49.185
the analyst feedback can be

00:55:49.186 --> 00:55:52.640
really powerful at least for this LODA algorithm.

00:55:52.641 --> 00:55:55.835
But now, let me speculate about

00:55:55.836 --> 00:55:59.170
why this might be overly optimistic.

00:55:59.171 --> 00:56:02.265
We have to remember that these datasets that we've

00:56:02.266 --> 00:56:03.655
constructed here are constructed from

00:56:03.656 --> 00:56:05.974
the supervised learning datasets.

00:56:05.975 --> 00:56:07.905
What's going on when we

00:56:07.906 --> 00:56:09.985
reweight these different projections,

00:56:09.986 --> 00:56:12.980
we're essentially reweighting the original features

00:56:12.981 --> 00:56:15.650
and putting more weight on some than on others.

00:56:15.651 --> 00:56:18.915
So, it could be that because our anomalies are coming

00:56:18.916 --> 00:56:22.250
from classes in the classification problem,

00:56:22.251 --> 00:56:25.155
they were presumably determined to be classes

00:56:25.156 --> 00:56:26.755
because they were believed to be

00:56:26.756 --> 00:56:28.960
separable using the features.

00:56:28.961 --> 00:56:31.895
It may be they were

00:56:31.896 --> 00:56:34.455
essentially in a sort of a sneaky way of

00:56:34.456 --> 00:56:37.435
learning a classifier and taking advantage of that.

00:56:37.436 --> 00:56:39.905
So, it's not clear in

00:56:39.906 --> 00:56:42.485
a real fraud detection case would we get

00:56:42.486 --> 00:56:45.925
this same factor of two improvements.

00:56:45.926 --> 00:56:49.845
However, we have a paper under review right now at KDD,

00:56:49.846 --> 00:56:52.135
where we're doing reweighting for

00:56:52.136 --> 00:56:56.245
Isolation Forest using a sort

00:56:56.246 --> 00:56:57.785
of online learning strategy.

00:56:57.786 --> 00:56:59.780
We have applied this to

00:56:59.781 --> 00:57:03.810
this advanced persistent threat cybersecurity case,

00:57:03.811 --> 00:57:05.575
which is not a supervised learning case,

00:57:05.576 --> 00:57:08.065
and we're still seeing a substantial,

00:57:08.066 --> 00:57:10.065
not a factor two, but a substantial benefit

00:57:10.066 --> 00:57:13.050
from integrating the feedback. Yes.

00:57:13.051 --> 00:57:15.010
&gt;&gt; Would it help to put

00:57:15.011 --> 00:57:17.555
the label to answer that question?

00:57:17.556 --> 00:57:18.225
&gt;&gt; Labeled?

00:57:18.226 --> 00:57:19.885
&gt;&gt; So you have your classes.

00:57:19.886 --> 00:57:20.155
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:57:20.156 --> 00:57:21.795
&gt;&gt; Would you put some label noise,

00:57:21.796 --> 00:57:24.310
it's going to start mixing the classes,

00:57:24.311 --> 00:57:25.465
they wouldn't be separable anymore.

00:57:25.466 --> 00:57:32.359
&gt;&gt; Oh, you mean when we constructed, that's interesting.

00:57:32.360 --> 00:57:34.155
That's interesting. We should maybe look at that.

00:57:34.156 --> 00:57:37.155
That would be a good test at least for this particularly.

00:57:40.315 --> 00:57:44.155
Yeah. I guess another thing is,

00:57:44.156 --> 00:57:48.535
we could create benchmarks where our nominal points

00:57:48.536 --> 00:57:50.415
come from say just one class and

00:57:50.416 --> 00:57:51.775
the anomalies are from the mixture

00:57:51.776 --> 00:57:53.855
of all the other classes.

00:57:54.485 --> 00:57:57.395
Or maybe we should use lots

00:57:57.396 --> 00:57:59.425
of each classes so that there's

00:57:59.426 --> 00:58:01.265
probably not a simple decision boundary that

00:58:01.266 --> 00:58:03.635
separates the nominals from the anomalies,

00:58:03.636 --> 00:58:06.395
because they'll be very multi-modal distributions

00:58:06.396 --> 00:58:07.515
on both sides. Yeah.

00:58:07.516 --> 00:58:09.850
&gt;&gt; So the feedback you have here is

00:58:09.851 --> 00:58:13.335
very example driven right on the analyst judging thing.

00:58:13.336 --> 00:58:15.970
It seems like you can also order it to be feature-driven,

00:58:15.971 --> 00:58:19.215
or going back to pattern terminology, patterns.

00:58:19.216 --> 00:58:21.125
So in fraud cases,

00:58:21.126 --> 00:58:23.410
people are often interested in patterns like

00:58:23.411 --> 00:58:26.255
a small gas station purchase followed by big purchase,

00:58:26.256 --> 00:58:27.755
is that pattern right?

00:58:27.756 --> 00:58:29.395
And instead, the anomalies are

00:58:29.396 --> 00:58:31.960
evidence that this pattern exists.

00:58:31.961 --> 00:58:33.825
Have you thought about trying to

00:58:33.826 --> 00:58:35.435
order in other ways where people might be able to

00:58:35.436 --> 00:58:38.715
give a more sample-efficient feedback over

00:58:38.716 --> 00:58:40.315
an entire feature as to whether or

00:58:40.316 --> 00:58:42.885
not I believe something anomalous is happening here?

00:58:42.886 --> 00:58:45.365
&gt;&gt; We have not.

00:58:45.366 --> 00:58:49.095
We did try to do some sort of

00:58:49.096 --> 00:58:53.075
model-based work back in the Insider threat dataset,

00:58:53.076 --> 00:58:56.575
where we had some theories of Insider threats and we

00:58:56.576 --> 00:59:01.615
designed features specifically to look for those threats,

00:59:01.616 --> 00:59:04.765
like someone copying a large number of files

00:59:04.766 --> 00:59:10.145
onto a thumb drive.

00:59:10.146 --> 00:59:14.295
But you have in mind maybe more of a knowledge discovery

00:59:14.296 --> 00:59:19.715
setting where the analyst looks at our anomalies.

00:59:19.716 --> 00:59:23.885
Or maybe can you do like as we've been discussing today,

00:59:23.886 --> 00:59:27.275
maybe some cluster analysis of these anomalies

00:59:27.276 --> 00:59:30.865
the way you guys are clustering mistakes and say,

00:59:30.866 --> 00:59:32.655
"Oh, I see sort of a pattern in this,

00:59:32.656 --> 00:59:34.235
and this looks completely bogus to me.

00:59:34.236 --> 00:59:37.290
This looks like just some side effect of some instrument.

00:59:37.291 --> 00:59:38.875
So, I don't want to see any more

00:59:38.876 --> 00:59:40.295
of these cases like this,

00:59:40.296 --> 00:59:43.125
for instance, or this looks interesting to me.

00:59:43.126 --> 00:59:45.620
So, everything that looks like this,

00:59:45.621 --> 00:59:48.565
let me label that whole cluster." Yeah.

00:59:48.566 --> 00:59:50.910
&gt;&gt; Okay. How you select the example that they set

00:59:50.911 --> 00:59:53.290
them in kind of set based criterion right.

00:59:53.291 --> 00:59:55.035
If you want to exclude a whole feature,

00:59:55.036 --> 00:59:56.855
if you just randomly select examples,

00:59:56.856 --> 00:59:58.335
chances are you won't exclude that feature.

00:59:58.336 --> 01:00:00.235
Whereas if you start selecting them in conjunction,

01:00:00.236 --> 01:00:01.555
you can do that.

01:00:01.556 --> 01:00:04.315
&gt;&gt; Yes. We did look at whether the sort

01:00:04.316 --> 01:00:07.300
of select the biggest anomaly is the right thing to do.

01:00:07.301 --> 01:00:10.785
And Weng-Keen, you may or may not know,

01:00:10.786 --> 01:00:12.195
but when he was a graduate student,

01:00:12.196 --> 01:00:15.160
he was working on rare category discovery and

01:00:15.161 --> 01:00:18.925
there you do have some diversity tricks.

01:00:18.926 --> 01:00:22.555
So we could think of this as also,

01:00:22.556 --> 01:00:24.240
could we elicit from the expert.

01:00:24.241 --> 01:00:25.495
Again, consistent with some of

01:00:25.496 --> 01:00:26.905
the things I've been hearing about here.

01:00:26.906 --> 01:00:28.665
Not only is that anomaly,

01:00:28.666 --> 01:00:30.505
but I'm going to call that a full

01:00:30.506 --> 01:00:33.035
anomaly because this corresponds

01:00:33.036 --> 01:00:34.600
to a kind of use-case I know.

01:00:34.601 --> 01:00:37.755
This is the check that the credit card is working before

01:00:37.756 --> 01:00:41.850
you use it pattern.

01:00:41.851 --> 01:00:44.675
No, we haven't thought about that,

01:00:44.676 --> 01:00:49.840
but it's a great idea obviously. Yes.

01:00:49.841 --> 01:00:52.375
&gt;&gt; Can you still reward diversity,

01:00:52.376 --> 01:00:54.575
because diversity will definitely change

01:00:54.576 --> 01:00:57.525
the efficacy of query versus anomaly.

01:00:57.526 --> 01:00:59.935
So, it seems like your quick trick

01:00:59.936 --> 01:01:01.720
that can completely change your metric,

01:01:01.721 --> 01:01:03.075
or is that not true?

01:01:03.076 --> 01:01:05.575
&gt;&gt; No, we would really have to

01:01:05.576 --> 01:01:09.605
also have something in our metric that rewards diversity.

01:01:09.606 --> 01:01:10.895
Well, as you say,

01:01:10.896 --> 01:01:12.955
if we could essentially present

01:01:12.956 --> 01:01:16.380
a group of proposed anomalies,

01:01:16.381 --> 01:01:18.555
and say we think these points are all

01:01:18.556 --> 01:01:21.960
anomalous for the same reason, are we right?

01:01:21.961 --> 01:01:25.640
And get feedback on the whole group at once,

01:01:25.641 --> 01:01:28.170
then that would be great.

01:01:28.171 --> 01:01:29.585
Then the next query,

01:01:29.586 --> 01:01:31.205
we would use obviously a different group.

01:01:31.206 --> 01:01:33.495
But we might have to balance them,

01:01:33.496 --> 01:01:35.389
I know in real situations.

01:01:35.390 --> 01:01:36.475
I remember years ago,

01:01:36.476 --> 01:01:39.775
I visited Bell Labs and Darrell [inaudible] was working

01:01:39.776 --> 01:01:43.965
on payphone fraud for, well,

01:01:43.966 --> 01:01:47.620
it wasn't even AT&amp;T at the time,

01:01:47.621 --> 01:01:50.975
and they had some statistical model

01:01:50.976 --> 01:01:52.885
and they were looking for weird behavior.

01:01:52.886 --> 01:01:55.790
But they also ranked them by how valuable they were

01:01:55.791 --> 01:01:59.255
in terms of the financial impact of those frauds.

01:01:59.256 --> 01:02:02.085
So, that was it.

01:02:02.086 --> 01:02:04.795
Okay, well, I've got to

01:02:04.796 --> 01:02:06.585
move along because we're really behind.

01:02:06.586 --> 01:02:08.905
So, let's talk about these two applications.

01:02:08.906 --> 01:02:12.515
So the first one is, data cleaning and weather networks.

01:02:12.516 --> 01:02:15.315
So, I'm part of a group

01:02:15.316 --> 01:02:16.935
that's working on something called

01:02:16.936 --> 01:02:19.915
the Trans-African Hydro-Meteorological Observatory,

01:02:19.916 --> 01:02:23.475
and it's founded by two folks.

01:02:23.476 --> 01:02:25.440
One is a colleague of mine John Selker,

01:02:25.441 --> 01:02:27.795
who's a hydrologist and

01:02:27.796 --> 01:02:29.435
the other is a guy named Nick van de Giesen,

01:02:29.436 --> 01:02:32.955
who's at the Technical University of Delft,

01:02:32.956 --> 01:02:35.135
also a hydrologist.

01:02:35.136 --> 01:02:35.705
They were at

01:02:35.706 --> 01:02:37.715
some World Meteorological Organization meeting,

01:02:37.716 --> 01:02:41.615
at which this plot was put up showing where are

01:02:41.616 --> 01:02:43.825
the weather stations around the world that reliably

01:02:43.826 --> 01:02:46.550
report data to the World Meteorological Organization.

01:02:46.551 --> 01:02:49.215
Those purple blue dots are the ones

01:02:49.216 --> 01:02:51.825
that are the good weather stations.

01:02:51.826 --> 01:02:57.000
We can see that in most of Latin America and Africa,

01:02:57.001 --> 01:03:00.740
the weather infrastructure is just very poor.

01:03:00.741 --> 01:03:09.140
So, in the context of agriculture in Africa,

01:03:09.141 --> 01:03:11.485
most agriculture is rain-fed,

01:03:11.486 --> 01:03:14.795
there's not a lot of irrigation.

01:03:15.085 --> 01:03:18.630
They noted that when you have very poor sensing,

01:03:18.631 --> 01:03:21.110
then you can't buy crop insurance

01:03:21.111 --> 01:03:22.415
because the insurance companies

01:03:22.416 --> 01:03:23.905
don't know what risk they would take.

01:03:23.906 --> 01:03:26.880
They don't know how to price the insurance.

01:03:26.881 --> 01:03:28.725
So, that leaves the farmers to

01:03:28.726 --> 01:03:30.690
plant their plants very far apart,

01:03:30.691 --> 01:03:32.525
so that each plant under

01:03:32.526 --> 01:03:34.515
the worst case precipitation conditions will

01:03:34.516 --> 01:03:37.005
still get enough water to do well.

01:03:37.006 --> 01:03:39.645
There have been field trials showing that if

01:03:39.646 --> 01:03:42.170
you do offer crop insurance,

01:03:42.171 --> 01:03:44.335
then the farmers plant much more aggressively,

01:03:44.336 --> 01:03:46.800
plant their plants much closer together and

01:03:46.801 --> 01:03:49.855
get a much higher agricultural productivity as a result.

01:03:49.856 --> 01:03:51.615
So, this is one of these examples

01:03:51.616 --> 01:03:53.665
of if we could provide better data,

01:03:53.666 --> 01:03:56.825
this could lead to crop insurance industry,

01:03:56.826 --> 01:03:59.005
which could in turn

01:03:59.006 --> 01:04:01.725
lead to higher agricultural productivity.

01:04:02.165 --> 01:04:04.825
John Selker is not a modest man.

01:04:04.826 --> 01:04:07.565
He wants to have Africa leapfrog over

01:04:07.566 --> 01:04:09.015
the whole rest of the world and become

01:04:09.016 --> 01:04:10.750
the best sensed continents.

01:04:10.751 --> 01:04:13.265
Of course, the price of sensors is dropping rapidly,

01:04:13.266 --> 01:04:14.755
and we can now build

01:04:14.756 --> 01:04:17.300
weather stations that are very cheap.

01:04:17.301 --> 01:04:19.825
So, we're trying to deploy 20,000 such stations

01:04:19.826 --> 01:04:22.990
across Africa and then create various data products.

01:04:22.991 --> 01:04:25.005
I like to say, "Well,

01:04:25.006 --> 01:04:28.225
weather stations are really cool, but for some reason,

01:04:28.226 --> 01:04:30.435
people want to put them out in the weather and expose

01:04:30.436 --> 01:04:32.175
them to the rain

01:04:32.176 --> 01:04:34.350
and all kinds of problems, and then they break."

01:04:34.351 --> 01:04:38.580
So, this is a kind of Internet of Things project.

01:04:38.581 --> 01:04:40.265
I like to say, the Internet of Things is

01:04:40.266 --> 01:04:41.975
going to be the Internet or broken things.

01:04:41.976 --> 01:04:44.025
So, how can we automatically

01:04:44.026 --> 01:04:45.885
detect which stations have

01:04:45.886 --> 01:04:48.235
failed, which sensors have failed?

01:04:48.605 --> 01:04:51.005
So, we might approach this as

01:04:51.006 --> 01:04:52.795
a sort of medical diagnosis problem.

01:04:52.796 --> 01:04:54.815
If we could say, "Well, these sensors

01:04:54.816 --> 01:04:57.160
tend to get one in five different diseases,

01:04:57.161 --> 01:04:59.615
can we learn to recognize the symptoms?"

01:04:59.616 --> 01:05:03.365
But this is one of those cases where we feel like

01:05:03.366 --> 01:05:05.125
there seems to be

01:05:05.126 --> 01:05:06.285
an unbounded number of ways

01:05:06.286 --> 01:05:08.245
these weather systems can fail.

01:05:08.246 --> 01:05:11.485
So, where there are known symptoms,

01:05:11.486 --> 01:05:12.905
we can write rules for them

01:05:12.906 --> 01:05:15.120
or build probabilistic models,

01:05:15.121 --> 01:05:16.985
but we want to use anomaly detection

01:05:16.986 --> 01:05:19.700
to detect new kinds of failures.

01:05:19.701 --> 01:05:22.295
So, I think in

01:05:22.296 --> 01:05:23.485
the interest of time I won't go

01:05:23.486 --> 01:05:25.135
through all the technical stuff here.

01:05:25.136 --> 01:05:27.665
But I'll just say that we are combining

01:05:27.666 --> 01:05:31.545
anomaly detection scores in a dynamic Bayesian network,

01:05:31.546 --> 01:05:33.800
where we reason about the state of each sensors.

01:05:33.801 --> 01:05:35.535
Then we're doing a foreign map inference

01:05:35.536 --> 01:05:36.615
to try to say given

01:05:36.616 --> 01:05:38.415
the observations that we've

01:05:38.416 --> 01:05:41.470
made turned into anomalies scores,

01:05:41.471 --> 01:05:46.745
we try to not just flag the data with anomaly flags,

01:05:46.746 --> 01:05:49.205
but actually do some root cause analysis.

01:05:49.206 --> 01:05:53.825
And say the reason that you got these 2,000 alarms,

01:05:53.826 --> 01:05:55.805
is due to one particular thermometer

01:05:55.806 --> 01:05:57.565
failing for some time interval.

01:05:57.566 --> 01:05:59.965
So, I guess I should say,

01:05:59.966 --> 01:06:04.745
maybe the most interesting or weirdest move here is

01:06:04.746 --> 01:06:06.535
a typical problem you have when you

01:06:06.536 --> 01:06:10.915
try to do diagnosis on this,

01:06:10.916 --> 01:06:13.085
is you want to model the sensor readings as a function

01:06:13.086 --> 01:06:16.370
of the state of the sensors.

01:06:16.371 --> 01:06:19.200
But these sensor distributions can be very messy,

01:06:19.201 --> 01:06:22.565
and so we replace the actual distribution sensor readings

01:06:22.566 --> 01:06:26.470
by the anomaly score assigned to those sensor readings.

01:06:26.471 --> 01:06:28.445
This has a nice virtue

01:06:28.446 --> 01:06:31.035
of normalizing everything to be in zero and one,

01:06:31.036 --> 01:06:34.785
and having a more predictable distribution.

01:06:34.786 --> 01:06:36.735
At least that's our hypothesis,

01:06:36.736 --> 01:06:40.355
but we don't have definitive results to prove this.

01:06:40.356 --> 01:06:43.425
So, I'll say, we're currently working

01:06:43.426 --> 01:06:46.905
a lot on comparing sensor readings at multiple stations.

01:06:46.906 --> 01:06:49.765
So then the other problem which is more related to

01:06:49.766 --> 01:06:52.825
open world is Open Category Classification.

01:06:52.826 --> 01:06:55.695
So, given that I have training data for say

01:06:55.696 --> 01:06:57.175
K classes that I've

01:06:57.176 --> 01:06:58.775
collected and I trained

01:06:58.776 --> 01:07:00.395
a multi-class classifier for them,

01:07:00.396 --> 01:07:02.045
but then my test data may contain

01:07:02.046 --> 01:07:03.995
queries corresponding to new classes,

01:07:03.996 --> 01:07:07.310
how can I detect them?

01:07:07.311 --> 01:07:09.445
We call them alien queries,

01:07:09.446 --> 01:07:11.805
the queries that belong to new kinds of objects.

01:07:11.806 --> 01:07:14.905
So, our approach is, given our training data,

01:07:14.906 --> 01:07:17.080
we train both our multi-class classifier

01:07:17.081 --> 01:07:18.885
but also an anomaly detector.

01:07:18.886 --> 01:07:21.130
So, then when an input query comes in,

01:07:21.131 --> 01:07:22.605
we look at the anomaly score.

01:07:22.606 --> 01:07:23.965
And if it's greater than some threshold,

01:07:23.966 --> 01:07:26.385
then we reject that point and say,

01:07:26.386 --> 01:07:28.675
"We think this might belong to a new category,

01:07:28.676 --> 01:07:30.275
so we're not going to classify it."

01:07:30.276 --> 01:07:31.725
But otherwise, we feed through

01:07:31.726 --> 01:07:34.590
our classifier and make our prediction.

01:07:34.591 --> 01:07:37.285
So, this was for me was

01:07:37.286 --> 01:07:40.255
motivated by another ecological problem which was,

01:07:40.256 --> 01:07:43.665
for about a decade I was working with some folks at

01:07:43.666 --> 01:07:45.870
the EPA and on campus

01:07:45.871 --> 01:07:49.265
at freshwater stream health monitoring.

01:07:49.266 --> 01:07:51.965
So the EPA every year in the United States does

01:07:51.966 --> 01:07:55.869
randomized sampling of freshwater streams,

01:07:55.870 --> 01:07:57.645
and what they do is they use a kick-net,

01:07:57.646 --> 01:07:59.375
which is what these guys are doing here,

01:07:59.376 --> 01:08:02.020
to collect insect larvae that

01:08:02.021 --> 01:08:05.255
live on the substrate of the streams,

01:08:05.256 --> 01:08:08.570
and then they bring those specimens into the lab.

01:08:08.571 --> 01:08:11.365
Currently, they manually identify

01:08:11.366 --> 01:08:14.145
them to genus or species.

01:08:14.146 --> 01:08:17.050
But this is very time consuming and so we thought, "Well,

01:08:17.051 --> 01:08:19.820
we'll build a robotic device that

01:08:19.821 --> 01:08:22.970
can singulate the specimens as they say,

01:08:22.971 --> 01:08:24.195
and then photograph them,

01:08:24.196 --> 01:08:28.370
and then we'll use Computer Vision to tell them apart.

01:08:28.371 --> 01:08:32.595
We did this back in the mid 2000s when everything

01:08:32.596 --> 01:08:34.695
was Bag of SIFT and Support Vector

01:08:34.696 --> 01:08:37.135
Machines as the classification technology.

01:08:37.136 --> 01:08:39.925
But we got up into the low 90 percent correct,

01:08:39.926 --> 01:08:42.590
over ultimately 54 categories.

01:08:42.591 --> 01:08:44.170
So we were all excited about that

01:08:44.171 --> 01:08:46.065
and then we started to look at actually deploying it,

01:08:46.066 --> 01:08:48.445
and we realized although our collaborators had

01:08:48.446 --> 01:08:50.415
collected specimens and we had

01:08:50.416 --> 01:08:52.920
these beautiful training data for all the specimens,

01:08:52.921 --> 01:08:56.090
the test samples are going to contain other stuff.

01:08:56.091 --> 01:08:59.265
It turns out more than 29 species

01:08:59.266 --> 01:09:01.285
or more than 54 species out there,

01:09:01.286 --> 01:09:04.125
like it's estimated there's something like 1,200 species

01:09:04.126 --> 01:09:08.405
of freshwater macro invertebrates as they call them.

01:09:08.406 --> 01:09:11.690
So this led us to think about,

01:09:11.691 --> 01:09:14.105
how could we deal with this problem?

01:09:14.106 --> 01:09:15.945
Of course, more recently in Computer Vision,

01:09:15.946 --> 01:09:17.960
this has become a popular problem.

01:09:17.961 --> 01:09:22.185
So, there are several techniques

01:09:22.186 --> 01:09:24.745
that are out there but as far as we know,

01:09:24.746 --> 01:09:26.435
no one has looked at the question whether we can

01:09:26.436 --> 01:09:28.780
provide any kind of theoretical guarantees.

01:09:28.781 --> 01:09:32.245
So, we are looking at a set up now,

01:09:32.246 --> 01:09:33.945
where we have two training sets.

01:09:33.946 --> 01:09:37.145
One training set is of our clean training data.

01:09:37.146 --> 01:09:39.165
Our collaborators went out and

01:09:39.166 --> 01:09:41.285
collected all these training data for us.

01:09:41.286 --> 01:09:42.550
It's all nicely labeled,

01:09:42.551 --> 01:09:45.290
and we know there are no contaminating samples in these.

01:09:45.291 --> 01:09:47.505
But in addition, we're going to require that we have

01:09:47.506 --> 01:09:50.275
a unlabeled collection of

01:09:50.276 --> 01:09:53.315
samples that are representative of our test queries.

01:09:53.316 --> 01:09:54.685
So, here, we are making

01:09:54.686 --> 01:09:57.115
a well-defined anomaly distribution

01:09:57.116 --> 01:09:59.265
assumption that we can get

01:09:59.266 --> 01:10:01.760
a sample from the distribution of anomalies.

01:10:01.761 --> 01:10:04.025
In fact, even talk about what do we mean to have

01:10:04.026 --> 01:10:06.105
a pat guarantee on

01:10:06.106 --> 01:10:09.380
detecting say 99 percent of all the aliens,

01:10:09.381 --> 01:10:11.525
right there, I'm assuming that's

01:10:11.526 --> 01:10:15.345
a sensible statement that there is such a distribution.

01:10:15.346 --> 01:10:20.045
So, we're going to assume that this contaminated sample,

01:10:20.046 --> 01:10:23.775
which we'll call SM for mixture is a mixture where again,

01:10:23.776 --> 01:10:26.115
the proportion of the aliens is Alpha.

01:10:26.116 --> 01:10:28.280
So, this density is a mixture of

01:10:28.281 --> 01:10:30.555
one minus Alpha times the clean distribution,

01:10:30.556 --> 01:10:36.235
p_not times Alpha times this anomaly distribution.

01:10:36.236 --> 01:10:39.120
Now, what we want to find is,

01:10:39.121 --> 01:10:42.530
if let's suppose this is the CDF of

01:10:42.531 --> 01:10:46.780
the anomaly scores of the anomaly points.

01:10:46.781 --> 01:10:48.595
So, we're really interested in,

01:10:48.596 --> 01:10:50.615
say we wanted to detect 95 percent of

01:10:50.616 --> 01:10:53.115
the aliens or anomalies,

01:10:53.116 --> 01:10:55.390
then we want to know this quantile.

01:10:55.391 --> 01:10:58.395
The anomaly score, such

01:10:58.396 --> 01:10:59.585
that 95 percent of

01:10:59.586 --> 01:11:01.450
the probability mass is greater than that.

01:11:01.451 --> 01:11:03.815
So we'd like to estimate this tau at

01:11:03.816 --> 01:11:06.505
0.05 or 0.01 or whatever we want.

01:11:06.506 --> 01:11:10.520
So we'll call this q, quantile 0.05.

01:11:10.521 --> 01:11:13.445
So, we note that if this is

01:11:13.446 --> 01:11:14.955
a mixture distribution since

01:11:14.956 --> 01:11:16.485
we're in one-dimensional world,

01:11:16.486 --> 01:11:17.985
it's true that for

01:11:17.986 --> 01:11:19.695
the cumulative distribution functions also,

01:11:19.696 --> 01:11:21.395
that the CDF of

01:11:21.396 --> 01:11:23.985
the anomaly scores for the mixture distribution is

01:11:23.986 --> 01:11:26.115
a convex combination of

01:11:26.116 --> 01:11:29.170
the nominal distribution in the anomaly distribution.

01:11:29.171 --> 01:11:30.800
So if you do a little algebra,

01:11:30.801 --> 01:11:34.315
you can say, GD, anomaly CDF can be calculated.

01:11:34.316 --> 01:11:36.365
We can estimate this from data.

01:11:36.366 --> 01:11:38.065
We can estimate this from data.

01:11:38.066 --> 01:11:39.375
If someone will just tell us

01:11:39.376 --> 01:11:44.255
Alpha or give us maybe a reasonable upper bound on Alpha,

01:11:44.256 --> 01:11:47.230
then we can use that to estimate this CDF.

01:11:47.231 --> 01:11:49.470
So, the picture looks something like this.

01:11:49.471 --> 01:11:52.750
This might be the CDF of the clean data,

01:11:52.751 --> 01:11:54.465
the black one is the mixture data,

01:11:54.466 --> 01:11:57.210
and maybe this is our anomaly CDF.

01:11:57.211 --> 01:11:58.725
So, we're going to set our threshold

01:11:58.726 --> 01:12:01.575
somewhere around in here maybe.

01:12:01.576 --> 01:12:03.455
We'll get all the anomalies,

01:12:03.456 --> 01:12:06.065
but we will have some number of false alarms too.

01:12:06.066 --> 01:12:07.960
So, of course,

01:12:07.961 --> 01:12:10.695
this all depends on how good the anomaly detector is,

01:12:10.696 --> 01:12:12.265
and how well they can distinguish

01:12:12.266 --> 01:12:16.405
the aliens from the known classes.

01:12:16.406 --> 01:12:19.760
So, when we look in practice,

01:12:19.761 --> 01:12:24.310
this is empirical CDF of the of the clean data,

01:12:24.311 --> 01:12:27.385
this is the empirical CDF of the mixture data,

01:12:27.386 --> 01:12:29.685
and of course, we're kind of subtracting them.

01:12:29.686 --> 01:12:32.285
So, unfortunately, this thing can go negative.

01:12:32.286 --> 01:12:35.725
This is the estimated CDF of the anomalies.

01:12:35.726 --> 01:12:37.610
So, it's pretty sad.

01:12:37.611 --> 01:12:39.825
But if we have a larger sample,

01:12:39.826 --> 01:12:41.655
it starts to look reasonable.

01:12:41.656 --> 01:12:44.865
But it still can cross this threshold a few times,

01:12:44.866 --> 01:12:46.135
and so we look at

01:12:46.136 --> 01:12:49.275
the largest value of the anomaly score that

01:12:49.276 --> 01:12:54.875
crosses this 0.05 line and we set our threshold there.

01:12:54.876 --> 01:12:57.575
That's what this pseudocode says.

01:12:57.576 --> 01:13:01.465
Then we're able to get a guarantee that if

01:13:01.466 --> 01:13:03.375
the number of samples in

01:13:03.376 --> 01:13:06.205
these two datasets is equal to n,

01:13:06.206 --> 01:13:10.785
then the required sample size is something like this.

01:13:10.786 --> 01:13:13.165
We have one over Epsilon squared term,

01:13:13.166 --> 01:13:14.485
we have something that's sort of a one

01:13:14.486 --> 01:13:16.505
over Alpha squared term,

01:13:16.506 --> 01:13:17.985
and then we have this,

01:13:17.986 --> 01:13:19.335
which is a funny thing

01:13:19.336 --> 01:13:21.035
that comes from the fact that we're estimating

01:13:21.036 --> 01:13:24.005
two different cumulative distribution functions and

01:13:24.006 --> 01:13:27.740
there are some concentration results for CDFs.

01:13:27.741 --> 01:13:30.305
So, then with probability one minus Delta,

01:13:30.306 --> 01:13:31.775
the alien detection rate will be

01:13:31.776 --> 01:13:33.905
at least one minus q plus Epsilon.

01:13:33.906 --> 01:13:36.645
So once again, we need some elbow room

01:13:36.646 --> 01:13:39.995
in the form of some Epsilon.

01:13:39.996 --> 01:13:46.815
Anyway, so that's what we can do.

01:13:46.816 --> 01:13:50.045
Then experimentally, I didn't put the results here,

01:13:50.046 --> 01:13:52.070
but we have a paper under review for

01:13:52.071 --> 01:13:55.950
ICML where we also show experimental results for this.

01:13:55.951 --> 01:14:04.195
Of course, it's a loose bound but it is a bound,

01:14:04.196 --> 01:14:06.165
and it does work in practice.

01:14:06.166 --> 01:14:09.315
We see that for some reasonable things,

01:14:09.316 --> 01:14:11.485
so we have some results

01:14:11.486 --> 01:14:16.845
on some image datasets and some benchmark datasets.

01:14:16.846 --> 01:14:19.215
We get false alarm rates using

01:14:19.216 --> 01:14:22.000
Isolation Forest as our anomaly detector.

01:14:22.001 --> 01:14:24.195
False alarm rates somewhere in

01:14:24.196 --> 01:14:29.854
the 10 to 20 percent range for reasonable sample sizes.

01:14:30.865 --> 01:14:33.725
So we're very happy with this, very pleased with it.

01:14:33.726 --> 01:14:36.135
So, to finish then.

01:14:36.245 --> 01:14:39.785
Outlier detection, I talked about the

01:14:39.786 --> 01:14:42.865
different unsupervised and mixed.

01:14:42.866 --> 01:14:44.675
Our benchmarking suggests that

01:14:44.676 --> 01:14:47.935
this Isolation Forest algorithm is quite nice.

01:14:49.165 --> 01:14:52.155
Surprisingly, that these percentile methods

01:14:52.156 --> 01:14:54.970
or quantile methods are not doing so well.

01:14:54.971 --> 01:14:57.430
I should say in passing,

01:14:57.431 --> 01:14:59.885
obviously these things don't work at

01:14:59.886 --> 01:15:02.175
all well by themselves for image data,

01:15:02.176 --> 01:15:03.810
we've kind of discussed this already,

01:15:03.811 --> 01:15:07.205
any kind of signal data or time series data.

01:15:07.206 --> 01:15:10.645
So, I think the next steps here,

01:15:10.646 --> 01:15:15.805
I'm very intrigued by things like GANs, and autoencoders,

01:15:15.806 --> 01:15:17.245
and other kind of manifold

01:15:17.246 --> 01:15:19.495
learning and density estimation techniques

01:15:19.496 --> 01:15:21.655
that are coming out of the deep learning literature,

01:15:21.656 --> 01:15:24.755
and so that's the next thing I want to try.

01:15:24.756 --> 01:15:26.455
There's some very nice papers at

01:15:26.456 --> 01:15:29.645
the last couple of NIPS about this,

01:15:29.646 --> 01:15:33.930
because I think that might give us

01:15:33.931 --> 01:15:36.645
much better anomaly detectors than we can get using

01:15:36.646 --> 01:15:40.090
these simple IAD feature based methods.

01:15:40.091 --> 01:15:42.375
Then we talked about the RPAD theory,

01:15:42.376 --> 01:15:44.445
which accounts for their behavior

01:15:44.446 --> 01:15:45.885
I think reasonably well.

01:15:45.886 --> 01:15:49.850
Then we talked about presenting

01:15:49.851 --> 01:15:51.145
the most important features,

01:15:51.146 --> 01:15:53.970
a simple ordered feature explanation

01:15:53.971 --> 01:15:55.995
incorporating expert feedback using

01:15:55.996 --> 01:15:58.170
this Accuracy at the Top strategy.

01:15:58.171 --> 01:16:03.080
Finally, guarantees for Open Category Detection.

01:16:03.081 --> 01:16:05.185
I apologize for running long,

01:16:05.186 --> 01:16:07.285
and thank my funders,

01:16:07.286 --> 01:16:09.165
and open it up for questions.

01:16:09.166 --> 01:16:10.855
&gt;&gt; Thanks a lot.

01:16:10.856 --> 01:16:21.460
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much. Yeah.

01:16:21.461 --> 01:16:23.925
&gt;&gt; So, at the beginning of the talk,

01:16:23.926 --> 01:16:26.015
you mentioned that there was a fundamental assumption

01:16:26.016 --> 01:16:28.205
about the nominal distribution

01:16:28.206 --> 01:16:29.550
and the anomaly distribution?

01:16:29.551 --> 01:16:33.810
&gt;&gt; Well, the assumption I wanted to call out is,

01:16:33.811 --> 01:16:36.085
is it safe in my application to assume that

01:16:36.086 --> 01:16:38.145
the anomalies come from a well-defined distribution.

01:16:38.146 --> 01:16:41.735
&gt;&gt; I'm curious, can you sketch how to weaken

01:16:41.736 --> 01:16:43.180
such an assumption to

01:16:43.181 --> 01:16:45.875
allow the fraudster also

01:16:45.876 --> 01:16:47.345
in the loop not just the analyst in the loop,

01:16:47.346 --> 01:16:48.880
but an arms race between

01:16:48.881 --> 01:16:50.205
the anomaly detector and

01:16:50.206 --> 01:16:52.070
the people who are trying to gain the thing.

01:16:52.071 --> 01:16:54.715
&gt;&gt; Well, so, I don't

01:16:54.716 --> 01:16:57.700
know how to do that in a theoretical framework.

01:16:57.701 --> 01:16:59.075
It seems to me that we end

01:16:59.076 --> 01:17:02.040
up having to think about it more like a game or

01:17:02.041 --> 01:17:07.095
minimaxing kind of adversarial learning setup instead,

01:17:07.096 --> 01:17:09.660
and try to get some guarantees that way,

01:17:09.661 --> 01:17:12.065
so that would be quite different from this.

01:17:12.066 --> 01:17:14.125
Of course, there is some interesting work

01:17:14.126 --> 01:17:16.555
in adversarial learning for supervised learning.

01:17:16.556 --> 01:17:19.085
Of course, everybody in deep nets right now

01:17:19.086 --> 01:17:20.105
is trying to do with adversarial

01:17:20.106 --> 01:17:22.435
examples in deep learning.

01:17:22.665 --> 01:17:25.435
Obviously, some of the strategies that are

01:17:25.436 --> 01:17:27.625
being explored like using GANs and

01:17:27.626 --> 01:17:30.605
trying to map queries onto the data

01:17:30.606 --> 01:17:35.560
manifold to strip out these adversarial attacks,

01:17:35.561 --> 01:17:37.725
many are very closely related to

01:17:37.726 --> 01:17:39.895
the same ideas of could we detect

01:17:39.896 --> 01:17:41.385
the adversarial queries as

01:17:41.386 --> 01:17:43.525
being outliers that fall

01:17:43.526 --> 01:17:45.680
outside of our data distribution.

01:17:45.681 --> 01:17:51.235
So, I think anomaly detection

01:17:51.236 --> 01:17:53.365
will again be part of the answer

01:17:53.366 --> 01:17:57.195
but this theory for

01:17:57.945 --> 01:18:02.040
open category will not be part of the answer there.

01:18:02.041 --> 01:18:05.285
We need some other more adversarial approach.

01:18:05.286 --> 01:18:07.605
&gt;&gt; And then just a clarification.

01:18:07.606 --> 01:18:09.735
Was the DARPA Atoms thing run

01:18:09.736 --> 01:18:12.290
as an arms race between red and blue team or was it?

01:18:12.291 --> 01:18:14.955
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so there was a red team that was

01:18:14.956 --> 01:18:19.010
actually based on people from CERT,

01:18:19.011 --> 01:18:21.355
the computer security guys.

01:18:21.356 --> 01:18:25.030
They have this whole White paper on

01:18:25.031 --> 01:18:27.205
different kinds of Insider threats in organizations that

01:18:27.206 --> 01:18:29.895
they've collected both from DOD and from industry.

01:18:29.896 --> 01:18:33.195
My favorite one is what they called the Insider Startup,

01:18:33.196 --> 01:18:35.310
that a sub-team decides,

01:18:35.311 --> 01:18:37.140
the company doesn't appreciate us,

01:18:37.141 --> 01:18:39.100
we're going to take the source tree with us

01:18:39.101 --> 01:18:42.200
and go form a startup.

01:18:42.201 --> 01:18:45.675
But there were other things,

01:18:45.676 --> 01:18:48.865
like someone who's passed over for promotion,

01:18:48.866 --> 01:18:51.185
survivor guilt, all these,

01:18:51.186 --> 01:18:53.075
and so they have this whole set of things,

01:18:53.076 --> 01:18:54.720
a lot of them very psychological.

01:18:54.721 --> 01:18:59.455
Then they hired a script writer to write scripts

01:18:59.456 --> 01:19:01.565
about what kinds of emails and text messages will

01:19:01.566 --> 01:19:02.935
these people be exchanging

01:19:02.936 --> 01:19:04.570
in addition to their desktop data,

01:19:04.571 --> 01:19:06.675
then they acted out all of this under

01:19:06.676 --> 01:19:09.475
this desktop and monitoring software,

01:19:09.476 --> 01:19:11.265
and then overlaid it on the behavior of

01:19:11.266 --> 01:19:13.935
real employees in the company.

01:19:13.936 --> 01:19:17.155
I had access to this in a very controlled setting,

01:19:17.156 --> 01:19:18.785
and we had to see if we could

01:19:18.786 --> 01:19:21.090
find those red team inserts.

01:19:21.091 --> 01:19:24.255
But unfortunately, we don't really know how hard they

01:19:24.256 --> 01:19:27.435
were and of course,

01:19:27.436 --> 01:19:28.785
we don't have access to the data anymore.

01:19:28.786 --> 01:19:30.030
It would have been interesting to,

01:19:30.031 --> 01:19:32.835
once we have the ground truth labels to go and see well.

01:19:32.836 --> 01:19:35.550
So how could we train a supervised learner,

01:19:35.551 --> 01:19:36.775
like go to the nearest neighbor

01:19:36.776 --> 01:19:37.565
classifier and figure this

01:19:37.566 --> 01:19:41.000
out quickly, we don't know.

01:19:41.001 --> 01:19:45.030
&gt;&gt; In the weather station example, I'm curious.

01:19:45.031 --> 01:19:47.575
Mostly, the anomaly detection algorithm you

01:19:47.576 --> 01:19:49.245
talked about seemed to be optimizing

01:19:49.246 --> 01:19:53.270
for low false alarm rates and high gadgets.

01:19:53.271 --> 01:19:54.965
But in some of the systems

01:19:54.966 --> 01:19:57.320
depending on how you're reacting to the anomaly,

01:19:57.321 --> 01:20:01.165
you might be able to tolerate lots of false alarms

01:20:01.166 --> 01:20:03.665
depending on what's important and how

01:20:03.666 --> 01:20:05.935
you're recovering from a problem.

01:20:05.936 --> 01:20:08.035
I was curious, in the weather station,

01:20:08.036 --> 01:20:09.435
what happens when you detect an anomaly?

01:20:09.436 --> 01:20:11.685
&gt;&gt; Well, so,

01:20:11.686 --> 01:20:15.765
in all existing weather networks that I'm aware of,

01:20:15.766 --> 01:20:18.695
they have a lot of flags and

01:20:18.696 --> 01:20:19.955
then they rely on some human

01:20:19.956 --> 01:20:21.775
eyeballs to look at them all.

01:20:21.776 --> 01:20:24.455
So for instance, Oklahoma Mesonet

01:20:24.456 --> 01:20:26.455
is the best weather network in the United States.

01:20:26.456 --> 01:20:27.665
They have a big budget, and they have

01:20:27.666 --> 01:20:31.650
four full-time meteorologists for 130 stations.

01:20:31.651 --> 01:20:33.975
So scaling that up to 20,000 stations in Africa,

01:20:33.976 --> 01:20:35.605
needed heck of a lot of.

01:20:35.606 --> 01:20:37.055
I just don't think that strategy

01:20:37.056 --> 01:20:38.455
scales to the Internet of Things so

01:20:38.456 --> 01:20:39.505
that's why I think we need to bring

01:20:39.506 --> 01:20:42.015
much more intelligence to bear.

01:20:42.016 --> 01:20:46.640
Also, most of the existing weather networks,

01:20:46.641 --> 01:20:51.505
they use basically hand-coded expert systems for writing

01:20:51.506 --> 01:20:55.075
these problem detection rules

01:20:55.076 --> 01:20:59.105
and so they are completely ad hoc.

01:20:59.165 --> 01:21:03.155
So, I think in some sense,

01:21:03.156 --> 01:21:05.305
low-hanging fruit for us because by

01:21:05.306 --> 01:21:08.040
bringing probabilistic inference on top of the alarming,

01:21:08.041 --> 01:21:10.410
we should be able to greatly.

01:21:10.411 --> 01:21:11.815
We still have lots of alarms,

01:21:11.816 --> 01:21:14.255
but we can say these 1000 alarms

01:21:14.256 --> 01:21:15.870
are really just due to one failure,

01:21:15.871 --> 01:21:18.950
and create a trouble ticket for it.

01:21:18.951 --> 01:21:22.340
Well, we're well on the way to building a system where

01:21:22.341 --> 01:21:26.395
the weather network manager,

01:21:26.396 --> 01:21:29.065
who everyday logs in and he gets a report of

01:21:29.066 --> 01:21:31.895
what are the new failures we found in the last 24 hours.

01:21:31.896 --> 01:21:34.030
Then he can very easily create trouble tickets and

01:21:34.031 --> 01:21:36.400
assign those to the field engineers,

01:21:36.401 --> 01:21:40.235
who then will be scheduled to go out and do the repairs.

01:21:40.236 --> 01:21:42.145
In our dreams, that would be integrated with

01:21:42.146 --> 01:21:44.500
an inventory system and on and on.

01:21:44.501 --> 01:21:47.240
But that's how they're set up.

01:21:47.241 --> 01:21:49.285
&gt;&gt; But you have to give a lot more knowledge

01:21:49.286 --> 01:21:52.350
than usually about how sensors failure

01:21:52.351 --> 01:21:55.515
essentially air models and

01:21:55.516 --> 01:21:58.460
must be not so much control you're aware off,

01:21:58.461 --> 01:22:01.340
where experts have very rich models about house,

01:22:01.341 --> 01:22:06.205
they would just use like this like doing weird things,

01:22:06.206 --> 01:22:08.820
but they filter in certain ways. [inaudible]

01:22:08.821 --> 01:22:11.155
&gt;&gt; A mature system,

01:22:11.156 --> 01:22:14.615
presumably, ultimately we find these recurring anomalies.

01:22:14.616 --> 01:22:17.110
So, then they bring them into the lab and say,

01:22:17.111 --> 01:22:18.950
"Okay, what's going on here?"

01:22:18.951 --> 01:22:22.675
Then, we've already had this happen.

01:22:22.676 --> 01:22:25.315
We didn't discover with our code,

01:22:25.316 --> 01:22:28.435
but there was a manufacturing defect

01:22:28.436 --> 01:22:30.355
or design defect in the station,

01:22:30.356 --> 01:22:32.215
and so all the generation two stations

01:22:32.216 --> 01:22:33.795
are having to be replaced with.

01:22:33.796 --> 01:22:38.730
But over time, you will accumulate a library of

01:22:38.731 --> 01:22:41.215
failure models and with

01:22:41.216 --> 01:22:44.360
very specialized detectors for those.

01:22:44.361 --> 01:22:46.875
But we want this anomaly infrastructure as

01:22:46.876 --> 01:22:49.730
a fallback because there's always something new.

01:22:49.731 --> 01:22:51.605
We haven't had any of our stations attacked by

01:22:51.606 --> 01:22:53.760
an elephant yet but I'm sure it'll happen.

01:22:53.761 --> 01:22:56.835
Oklahoma, they have unbelievable stories

01:22:56.836 --> 01:23:00.235
about cows getting into the thing.

01:23:01.905 --> 01:23:05.595
What is the symptom of a weather station where

01:23:05.596 --> 01:23:07.065
some ants have decided to lay

01:23:07.066 --> 01:23:09.645
eggs all over the circuit board?

01:23:09.646 --> 01:23:12.915
It's hard to have a disease model for that.

01:23:12.916 --> 01:23:17.245
&gt;&gt; I was just

01:23:17.246 --> 01:23:20.195
wondering about the online case of anomaly detection.

01:23:20.196 --> 01:23:22.345
You seem to assume here in the problem statement

01:23:22.346 --> 01:23:24.625
that the nominal distribution is stable over time.

01:23:24.626 --> 01:23:26.195
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, yes.

01:23:26.196 --> 01:23:26.985
&gt;&gt; What happens if

01:23:26.986 --> 01:23:28.875
the nominal distribution starts drifting

01:23:28.876 --> 01:23:30.070
over time because since

01:23:30.071 --> 01:23:31.505
the weighted identification changes,

01:23:31.506 --> 01:23:32.625
or whatever other issues?

01:23:32.626 --> 01:23:34.365
&gt;&gt; So, somehow we're going to

01:23:34.366 --> 01:23:37.355
have to track that distribution.

01:23:38.705 --> 01:23:42.145
Yeah, we haven't confronted that case here.

01:23:42.146 --> 01:23:44.585
Although in the weather data case,

01:23:44.586 --> 01:23:50.160
it's not stationary but

01:23:50.161 --> 01:23:51.435
it is very predictable in

01:23:51.436 --> 01:23:53.105
an annual cycle, on a daily cycle,

01:23:53.106 --> 01:23:55.230
there are lots of

01:23:55.231 --> 01:23:56.865
periodic components that you

01:23:56.866 --> 01:23:59.835
can sort of the traditional time-series techniques,

01:23:59.836 --> 01:24:02.390
model and pull them out of the signal.

01:24:02.391 --> 01:24:03.885
Then in your dream,

01:24:03.886 --> 01:24:08.175
you have now uncorrelated white noise and then it looks.

01:24:08.176 --> 01:24:11.840
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

01:24:11.841 --> 01:24:14.050
&gt;&gt; Maybe it's a part of the answer,

01:24:14.051 --> 01:24:19.005
but actually we've spent

01:24:19.006 --> 01:24:21.005
a whole year trying to do good feature engineering

01:24:21.006 --> 01:24:23.550
and decided this isn't working for us.

01:24:23.551 --> 01:24:26.880
Because it's just too much work to do all that modeling.

01:24:26.881 --> 01:24:28.960
When you make modeling errors,

01:24:28.961 --> 01:24:30.305
uses artifacts,

01:24:30.306 --> 01:24:33.385
and then we start making mistakes because of those.

01:24:33.386 --> 01:24:36.930
So, we're looking now at basically,

01:24:36.931 --> 01:24:39.515
looking at nearby weather stations that

01:24:39.516 --> 01:24:41.965
should be experiencing the same disturbances,

01:24:41.966 --> 01:24:44.155
the same annual daily cycles,

01:24:44.156 --> 01:24:47.080
and then comparing them.

01:24:47.081 --> 01:24:48.905
How well can we predict one using

01:24:48.906 --> 01:24:50.585
the other or what's their joint distribution,

01:24:50.586 --> 01:24:53.695
and using that to do the anomaly detection stuff.

01:24:53.696 --> 01:24:55.675
So, yeah.

01:24:55.676 --> 01:24:57.785
&gt;&gt; So you retrain the models,

01:24:57.786 --> 01:25:00.905
or there's some drift between predictions?

01:25:00.906 --> 01:25:03.805
&gt;&gt; Yes. Actually, we need to retrain

01:25:03.806 --> 01:25:05.445
the models really sort

01:25:05.446 --> 01:25:07.315
of a sliding window thing because again,

01:25:07.316 --> 01:25:09.105
the nature of correlations changes.

01:25:09.106 --> 01:25:11.585
In wintertime, for instance,

01:25:11.586 --> 01:25:15.860
precipitation is our most important variable,

01:25:15.861 --> 01:25:18.285
our most problematic sensor,

01:25:18.286 --> 01:25:21.135
and it has the smallest spatial scale

01:25:21.136 --> 01:25:22.620
in terms of spatial smoothness.

01:25:22.621 --> 01:25:24.005
In the wintertime, if we think

01:25:24.006 --> 01:25:25.630
about the Pacific Northwest,

01:25:25.631 --> 01:25:32.085
it's very smooth, very large storm.

01:25:32.086 --> 01:25:33.905
It affects the whole Pacific Northwest.

01:25:33.906 --> 01:25:35.535
So, if it's raining in one place,

01:25:35.536 --> 01:25:37.520
it's should be raining at another station

01:25:37.521 --> 01:25:39.530
and we can use that correlation.

01:25:39.531 --> 01:25:41.365
But in the summer time or in

01:25:41.366 --> 01:25:43.990
the late spring when we get these showers,

01:25:43.991 --> 01:25:47.219
stations becoming extremely uncorrelated,

01:25:47.220 --> 01:25:51.495
so then we need to have in a sense switch models to use

01:25:51.496 --> 01:25:53.040
a model that corresponds to

01:25:53.041 --> 01:25:55.835
a showery day with lots of little cells.

01:25:55.836 --> 01:25:58.355
Really, I think we need to be tracking that

01:25:58.356 --> 01:26:00.870
because it really is happening day-by-day weather.

01:26:00.871 --> 01:26:02.910
Like the first day of a rain event,

01:26:02.911 --> 01:26:06.295
it might have this large scale and then by the third day,

01:26:06.296 --> 01:26:09.150
it's into these little uncorrelated things.

01:26:09.151 --> 01:26:11.315
So, I think we actually need to

01:26:11.316 --> 01:26:14.245
attract that kind of larger spatial phenomenon.

01:26:14.246 --> 01:26:15.975
Speaking of larger spatial phenomenon,

01:26:15.976 --> 01:26:17.095
another thing is if you have

01:26:17.096 --> 01:26:20.925
a spatially clustered set

01:26:20.926 --> 01:26:23.170
of anomaly of alarms all ringing at once.

01:26:23.171 --> 01:26:24.775
It's probably a real weather event

01:26:24.776 --> 01:26:26.020
and an unusual weather event,

01:26:26.021 --> 01:26:27.705
not any of the stations broken,

01:26:27.706 --> 01:26:31.560
and that's knowledge it's not anywhere in our system,

01:26:31.561 --> 01:26:34.285
but the meteorologists definitely know this.

01:26:34.286 --> 01:26:35.655
Of course, there are other data sources

01:26:35.656 --> 01:26:37.085
like satellites, and radar,

01:26:37.086 --> 01:26:38.325
and things that we can

01:26:38.326 --> 01:26:41.555
use as additional sources of data for this, so.

01:26:41.556 --> 01:26:42.945
&gt;&gt; Okay. With that,

01:26:42.946 --> 01:26:45.000
we probably should thank. Thanks very much.

01:26:45.001 --> 01:26:49.475
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

